    "body": [
      "Minneapolis based Boiled In Lead isn't afraid to stray outside the normal boundaries of pop music, and their fifth release, Songs From The Gypsy, continues to prove that point. While that may be one of the reasons BiL hasn't broken out of the Minneapolis music yet, it should at the same time, be the reason they DO achieve national recognition. This is a finely crafted mix of celtic folk, hard rock, and world music. And if the music isn't reason enough for you to explore BiL further, this disc is an i-trax disc, which also contains the complete text for Steven Brust's book, on which this disc is based, in multimedia format. It is, to quote the bio information supplied - \"a dark, mystical tale of a gypsy and a series of mysterious crimes\" [I wish I had time to read the book]. Boiled In Lead's cast of characters include Adam Stemple on guitars and vocals, who along with Brust, co-wrote all the songs (except \"Ugros\", a traditional Hungarian melody) on the disc. Josef Kessler mans the fiddle, Robin \"Adnan\" Anders handles percussion, and Drew Miller adds his award winning bass and dulcimer. \"Raven, Owl, And I\" opens the disc with one of the celtic folksy cuts, sounding much like some of the old eclectic Jethro Tull, down to the \"Fat Man\" opening. Stemple's vocals shine on these rootsy songs. \"Leanan Sidhe\" takes another direction, with a heavy rock sound fueled by a strong base of screaching guitar and drums. \"Hide My Track\" mixes in a bit of both sounds, and some wonderful vocal help from Lojo Russo adds to the heavy feel. \"No Passenger\" slows it down again, and maybe I'm just too big a Tull fan, but that's who I keep hearing on the slower, Irish folksy cuts. \"The Gypsy\" has an electric Neil Young feel, including the hard guitar and selected feedback spots. Finishing up the disc are two more mellow cuts, the folksy \"Blackened Page\", and the relaxing acoustic \"The Fair Lady\", and then the off-the-wall \"Red Lights And Neon\", which is a strange mix of blues, fast rock, and muted, unintelligible vocals. I don't think BiL has done anything that will affect their large cult following, but as good as some of the songs here are, they're the kind that might cause the red flag to go up at commercial radio outlets. Stemple's vocals are best on the slower, celtic twinged pieces, and the heavier songs just don't do his voice justice. Robert Bogaert hails from Denver, by way of Chicago, and on this, his debut CD, puts his 25+ years of experience into a mad collection of guitar based folk-rock and barroom blues, with an occasional touch of self professed silliness. \"Daily Machine\" starts off this collection with the kind of song you'd be likely to find at your local open-mike bar, with the usual crowd clapping along in between shots of Old Granddad. Not one of the better moments of the disc, but fortunately, it gets better as Bogaert gets a little more serious. \"Time To Stop\" and \"Key Of Solomon\" are nice folky rockers that beg you to sing along. \"Rome\" has a faster pace, and one that Bogaert's voice strains to keep up with at times. \"Chip Off The Old Block\" is another barroom blues number about his coming to the realization that he is now like he remembered his father \"...now that I'm a full grown man I can almost match him beer for beer\". \"Friends Along The Way\" is my favorite among this diverse set, with a good message - \"you've just got to make up your mind to do the things you feel you need to and try to make some friends along the way\" - and soothing guitar track, supplimented by just the right mix of piano and Eaglesque backing harmonies. \"Anything For A Buck\" is a light hearted look at greed and the inane things that people, specifically Madonna and the kids of Fame, will do for the green - \"I'd like to get that material girl and chop her into pieces. Just throw the bloody carcass to the vultures...She dresses rude and she poses nude, I hear she made a porno movie, but of course it all was done in the name of culture.\" The closer, \"Wasted Acres\", has a country, sing along feel that you'll find yourself singing long after the disc has ended. The serious side of Bogaert is the dominant one on this disc, and that's a good thing. Bogaert succeeds in being a serious storyteller and songwriter, and the music is perfect for bringing the comradery of the local pub into your home. Just chill a few beers and have the gang over for a listen. But be ready for the sing along...\"All the animals eat Cat Chow in Wasted Acres\" Bone Jar calls their music \"unique adult alternative\", and unique is certainly a good word. The instrument list should grab your attention, with band members Zelda Blue playing dobro guitar, keyboards, percussion, saxophone, Japanese shakuhachi, and Max Tyrell handling lead guitar, dobro, electronic bowed guitar, bass, percussion, keyboards, and programming. Both members share lead and background vocal duties. Put all this together and you have a soothing combination of mellow John and Yoko, a little Everything But The Girl, some world beat rhythms, and a touch of Annie Lennox. The first two cuts are hypnotic and mesmerizing with their dark beats, surrounded by errily arranged instrumentation. \"War Paint\" is a little brighter, with nice vocal work by Blue. \"Tears\" is where the Lennon comparisons come to the surface, sounding like a continuation of the Double Fantasy album with its sensual vocal work and cutting background guitar work, and \"Congo Square\" could be from an earlier Lennon phase. \"Blackjack\" I know I've heard somewhere before, although no credits are given for the song in the liner notes. We get to drift off into our trance a little more in the middle of the disc before \"In The Morning\" picks up the pace a little with some neat keyboard and guitar work, creating an almost pop/dance soundbed. And where there's John, there's also Yoko, and \"Hush A Wild Wind\" features Ono-like warbling over an asian beat. \"Golden Wings\" closes the disc on a very fine note, with a Pink Floyd-ish sound that leaves you disappointed when it ends. \"Flowers From A Freaky World\" is the perfect compliment to a candlelit dinner with that special someone. Music that won't take over the room, but not let you forget it's there."
    ],
