    "body": [
      "David Cooper comes armed with an impressive musical history - as an award winning songwriter with credits from Norway, Australia, and Nashville, and as an international touring musician, opening shows for Don McLean and The Moody Blues. This disc has some notable names associated with it, from producer Michael Lord (Sir Mix-A-Lot), to musicians Ben Smith (drums - Heart), Libby Torrance (vocals - Nancy Wilson), Dewey Marler (sax - Ray Charles), and Garey Shelton (bass - Jesse Colin Young). Musically, Cooper cites influences such as Jim Croce and Dan Fogelberg, both of which are very evident in the music. I hear a very stong resemblence to Al Stewart throughout the disc, but especially in \"What Price, The Sky\". Cooper's music fits snugly into the mellow, adult-oriented catagory, with a variety of sweeping instrumentations from horns and keyboards, outstanding songwriting and equally well done vocals. The songs on the disc range draw on several styles, from folk to jazz and even a little country or blues from time to time. \"Headstones\" sounds eerily like Croce's \"Time In A Bottle\" with some flute thrown in for a nice touch, and \"I'm Sorry That I Never Made You Smile\", written to a coffeeshop waitress, is more Croce with jazzy additions. \"That's How They Make You A Man\" is another where the Stewart comparisons are strong, with Marler blowing a mean sax in the background. \"When It's All Said And Done\", has a nice blues/jazz arrangement. If I were still in radio, I'd pair up \"Who Do You Think You Are\" with the Eagles \"Get Over It\". This one, about those in serious need of an attitude adjustment, is more uptempo than the norm here, and features Rod Cook and his electric guitar. \"Lady In The Window\" is a haunting ballad about someone watching the world pass her by, with strong Stewart and Fogelberg influences. The closer, \"Mile After Mile\", although written in 1976, has a nice timeless sound that would probably do well on any of the generic lite-FM stations, even with the Style Council style keyboards towards the end. An impressive release indeed from Cooper. We all need a nice relaxing disc like this for those romantic dinners, or nights by the fireplace. Cooper writes and performs songs made just for those nights, where the music isn't the most important ingredient, but the right music adds just the right touch. Once upon a time, hunger and hope packed Irish and Scots into boats bound for America. What they met in its ports satisfied neither their hunger nor their hopes, and they looked to the mountains West. They and the music of their origins took root in those Appalachians. And in those mountains, hunger and hope were as firmly rooted as the people and their music. The children and their children passed down the music as they learned it and colored it with the dirt of their fields and the fog on the ridges. And the years passed while the Appalachians sang. Then one day, as the children of children from other ports cavorted electrically, there were some who could hear the singing in the mountains and they listened. They could hear that the singing was plain and pure, cousin to the edgy rock they midwifed from the blues still other ports had sent. Some, years before, called themselves the Dirt Band, others Pentangle. Time kept pace, the rock grew edgier, but the mountains still sang of dirt, fog, hope, and hunger. Among the next generation of children in rock, there were, among those who listened to the mountains, ones named Tom King, Tim Eriksen, and Peter Irvine. They listened and worked until what they sang kept a foot on the rocky edge with the other foot on the mountain. And they came to create \"Comet\", where they play hard, plain and pure songs borrowed from history, hymnals and lore. (And sometimes, they sing of Jersey City.) May they live and play happily ever after. Out of Seattle, of all places, comes K.J. Corye, bringing with her a powerful blend of country, blues, and ballads that makes you sit back and take notice. With music like this, there has to be some south in Corye's past, and sure enough, she's lived in North Dakota, Missouri, and Alabama on her way to Seattle. She may have learned to play guitar and violin as a youngster, but God blessed her with a powerful, soulful voice made just for the music she packs into her debut release. Corye mixes up the sound nicely from track to track, starting with the bluesy \"Kick Me\", about an all give, no get relationship. The country comes in next on the title track, featuring some nice slide work. The two ballads on the disc are very well done, especially \"If I Fall\", which is the standout cut on this fine collection. Also worth noting are the cover of Bobbie Gentry's \"Ode To Billie Joe\", and the satire laced, Jim-Croce-like \"Emerald Pie\", where money may not buy you love, but it sure buys alot of happiness."
    ],
