    "body": [
      "Out of New York comes Elizabeth Gone, calling themselves \"alternative acoustic rock\", and this tape is an impressive first demo. Led by singer/songwriter Vincent James on guitars, vocals, and keyboards, they meld the crisp guitar work of Indigo Girls, the eclectic instrumentation of Dream Acadamy, and the coffeehouse sound of Everything But The Girl into a soothing treat for the ears. James is joined by Maria Baugh (percussion/background vocals), Kim Milai (drums/flute), Rick Watson (bass), and Ralph Farris (violin/keyboards). James' voice flows smoothly through the twists and turns like a cross, oddly enough, between Vince Clark (Yaz) and Mary Chapin Carpenter. \"Far From Heaven\" and \"The 70's\" are both upbeat, catchy guitar melodies, and this is where Elizabeth Gone shines. \"Shine\" is another of the better cuts, with a great mix of percussion, flute, and violin. Farris' violin work also appears in the chilling \"The Train Song\", where it is the dominant instrument, and again in the refreshing \"A Picture In A Photograph\". \"City Lights\" has the hardest edge of any track, with the guitar again in the forefront. We listened to this a few times in front of the fire on our first chilly evening of the season, and I couldn't think of a more appropriate selection. Easy flowing, relaxing, and laid-back are some of the more overused adjectives I could think of for describing the sound here. If you're an acoustic fan, you won't be disappointed. Together around two years, Emote has forged a definate 90's sound, with a cleaner, crisper touch than your average Pearl Jam wannabe. Emote is Joe Riehl (vocals and bass), Doug Harrison (guitar), and Todd Woolsey (drums), with production handled quite nicely by someone or something known as Diode. \"Overflow\" is full of contemporary guitar powered tracks that would fit comfortably into any alternative radio station playlist. The first two cuts, \"Your Voice Still Haunts Me\" and \"Beautiful\" are full of Bob Mould (Sugar) influenced guitar mixed with good vocal work by Riehl, the latter with a heavy guitar solo by Harrison. \"The Bleeder\" shows Emote can slow down the pace without skipping a beat. \"Alone\" shows off some guitar and drum work that could pass for old Boston material. The highlight of the disc is \"Living For Tomorrow\", with a heavier edge and a sing-along-able bass dominated chorus. \"Babylon\" in an interesting cut, alternating between an almost metal feel, and an islandy, not quite reggae guitar base. \"Gecko On A Rockslide\" is a frantic rocker with a punk feel, along with some interestingly placed rockabilly guitar licks, that plays upon a common theme of love lost, found in several songs on the disc. Emote has a new release (9/95) entitled \" Suborganic \". If they follow the same musical path as \"Overflow\", I'm sure we'll be hearing from them commercially in the future. I'm not sure yet exactly why I like this band, but I do. A lot! \"Suborganic\" is their second independent release and it continues on the guitar laden, extremely catchy music that made their first release (\" Overflow \") so enjoyable. Either are a steal at $5 each, but if your budget is severely limited, spend the money for this one. The band is made up of Joe Riehl (vocals, guitar), Doug Harrison (lead guitar, bass), Todd Woolsey (drums), and Aaron Stine (bass, guitar). There's nothing fancy here, just lots of guitar and some real stick-in-your-head lyrics and rhythms. You'd never know it, but this disc was conceived and recorded in 24 hours. Our review of \"Overflow\" mentioned Bob Mould and Sugar, as well as Boston, and those same sounds are present here. So is the song \"Your Voice Still Haunts Me\", a carryover from the first disc. Also included is a cover song - \"That Little Girl\" from Riehl's dad's band (The Shadows). \"Blame\" is an interesting mix of grunge guitars and country riffs, that works surprisingly well. Already working on their third release - \"Phenome\" - Emote certainly has a bright future. They continue to put out music that encompasses all that is good in the 90's brand of \"alternative\"."
    ],
