    "body": [
      "When southern rock was redefined by the likes of REM, Dumptruck, and The Swimming Pool Q's in the early 80's, Mitch Easter was right in the middle of the mix. He can still be found down here, as of late working with this South Carolina band with a definate Beatles theme to their name and this particular release. \"Suitable For Framing\" is a misplaced opening track - its 80's cutout metal sound is not indicitive of what's to come. \"Starting Again\" gets things going in the right direction with a jazzy pop beat, then \"Character Flaw\" comes in with the signature Easter production, a sort of echoed mist floating around the sound. This one could easily be added to his early work with REM and no one would notice. Jangly guitar then takes over in \"Out Of School\" and \"That's What Life Is All About\", the latter of which features some White Album psychedelia. Side two is more of the same, starting with a couple of good power pop songs, \"Wait Around\" and \"Hampton Avenue\". The closer, \"Stairs Of Separation\", is another throwback to the Athens, GA sound that was so influenced by Easter. The only real bummer on this release is the recording quality, which was turned up a notch or two too high. I can picture the meters peaked, and it's very noticeable on the tape. Ike's in the White House, Lucky's in your rolled-up sleeve, Brylcreem keepin' your do doin', babe in the back of your ride and Dick Clark's got no clue where the REAL music is. But these boys do. See they got the word that it's in Bo Diddley's backbeat and Chuck Berry's guitar bends. And they got theirs from the boys in the smoky clubs making feet move between fights. And it works. Go ahead, big man, keep your Cuban heels still when Jimmy Sanchez starts tapping on \"Jump Start Funk\". Bet Dobie Gillis just don't get it when Steve Lucky says \"she got the money and I got the honey\" in \"Rich Woman\". It's time to Stroll when Rob Suddeth fires up his sax and Steve Gurr pours guitar gas on it in \"The Sweeper\". Tim Eschliman takes you downtown on slick vocals and bass in \"You Better Believe It\". And you better believe it. Believe it or Five Guys Named Mo (what they used to go by) will show you. And you're gonna like it. Ohhhhh, bayyyy-beeeee . . . . I don't know much about Tommy Rivers, as all we received was the tape and no bio or contact info. As for the music, Rivers doesn't try to follow any trends or be on the cutting edge with today's media darlings. What he does is write and play good old, small club rock and roll, with a sound that parallels old Bryan Adams or even the Stones. His vocals are delivered with attitude, and a rough quality at times similiar to Mike Peters, especially on \"Can't Believe What You Read\", which could easily be mistaken for an old Alarm song. The guitars are the instrument of focus throughout, but there are a couple times where they are toned down, with different results. On \"Tryin' Like A Fool\", Rivers rough vocal style works well, but on \"Already Know The Score\", he suffers without the backin of the full band."
    ],
