    "body": [
      "Try as some of you might, it's hard to avoid the recent resurgence in the power pop movement, with groups such as Gin Blossoms, The Rembrandts, even Matthew Sweet making waves in the charts and on the video channels. If these newcomers have grabbed your listening attention, keep reading. Not Lame Records has packaged into one nice package, what some labels would consider two releases, and that alone deserves kudos for giving us consumers a much needed break from underpackaging (you want me to pay full price for a 30 minute Green Day disc? I don't think so!). Included here are 17 songs, the first 9 of which are from The Rooks, a New York band already called \"one of the most important bands in the developing power pop empire\" by Billboard magazine. Their sound is very much like early Rembrandts, lots of catchy hooks and sweeping harmonies. There is enough variation from song to song that trying to compare The Rooks to only one band, or any one decade for that matter, is tough. With their music, The Rooks will certainly draw comparisons to artists as far back as the 60's Brit invasion, possibly some early 80's skinny tie bands, and a few from the 90's pop resurrection. The second half of the disc is a relatively new L.A. band, Twenty Cent Crush. Although, if not paying attention, you might miss the transition, the two bands do have a different sound. TCC draws more from the early 80's, with more guitar fed power than The Rooks. As always, there are exceptions. \"Tuesday Morning\" is the first obviously 60's influenced cut, a mellow love song that needs no additional sugar. For the most part, you can use either The Romantics or Squeeze as a \"sounds like\" for this promising newcomer. Is Not Lame a step ahead of the next movement in music? The foundation is there now, and with bands like The Rooks and Twenty Cent Crush coming, the road ahead may very well be paved with a refurbished brand of melodic pop groups. I could think of a lot worse directions to be heading. We all need some good clean fun in our collection. And I made it all the way through the review without mentioning The Beatles. Here's a five song EP from the same band that occupied my player for the better part of a week (see review for A Double Dose Of Pop). Granted, it would be tough to follow up such an energetic and delightful effort, but where every song from the other disc had me at least tapping my fingers, this one struggles for the first three tracks, and with only five it makes it hard to recover. It's not until \"Friends Of Mine\" that we hear the jangly guitars and the fun hooks that reeled me into The Rooks power pop world. The opener here is \"It's A Crying Shame\", and although the jangly guitars are there, it seems as if it's recorded too slow, and never gets out of the gate. \"House Of Fortune\" and \"War\" are slower, almost Moody Blues-ish tunes that somehow seem out of place based on their last release. A savior is the last cut, a nice Christmas song that will certainly assume a place in my short list of bearable holiday music. If your pop budget allows for only one purchase, my recommendation is to check out The Rooks other release first, then come back to this one to fill out your collection. It's hard to imagine a jazz pianist being inspired by the music of Hendrix, but Robins is such a breed. Not only is Robins an experienced pianist, but he has had success as an arranger, conductor, producer, and singer. Here, his interpretations of 20 classic Hendrix cuts from four albums (Are You Experienced?, Axis: Bold As Love, Electric Ladyland, and The Cry Of Love) may have more appeal to jazz or piano fans. Not being the biggest Hendrix fan, I turned the disc over to two people who are and the feedback was split. As a lover of piano, I find this collection very entertaining. More often than not, you will find yourself scurring for the CD case to identify the song, then pulling a V-8 once it all comes together. Robins' style is energetic, yet relaxed when needed, such as the ballads of Driftin' and Angel."
    ],
