    "body": [
      "In the span of two years, Third Stone has released two albums (\"The Stuff\" was their first) and appeared on the movie soundtrack for \"The Garbageman\". Plans are already in place for Third Stone to appear on the soundtrack for the forthcoming movie \"Dark Angel\" later this year. A national tour is also in the works for this summer and fall. Onto the disc, where Third Stone unleashes a successful combination of metal, melodies, and the occasional bluesy riff. I need to start by mentioning one of the best new songs I've heard in a while - \"Tomorrow\", a classic I-Love-You-Don't-You-Love-Me ballad that luckily is on CD instead of vinyl - the stylus would already have cut through like a pipe cutter. Vocalist Bryce Johnson sounds as comfortable doing the obligatory slow tunes as they do with the rockers. So much so that they've included three on this disc - \"Last Words\", \"Soft Spot\" and the aforementioned \"Tomorrow\". They also manage to show their sense of humor by including an answering machine message from the Ottawa HS politely declining their offer to play a fine arts festival. Seems the caller broke his back in the mosh pit the last time they played there! Tom Grassman shows off his lead guitar work best on \"Sixteenfive\", with slashing rhythms, including the killer intro, and Johnson chimes in with his best Ozzy Osbourne imitation. I haven't heard much from Tangerine Dream since the mid 80's. Anyone who saw the movie \"Risky Business\" will remember their sound. \"The Dream Mixes\" is collection of new material and remixed songs from \"Tyranny Of Beauty\", \"Rockoon\", and \"Turn Of The Tides.\" The older songs have been remixed for dancing with names like \"The Motown Monk Mix\" and \"The Break Freak Mix.\" What has always impressed me about Tangerine Dream is their diverse sound and thick textures of their songs. For those who don't know, Tangerine Dream consists of Jerome and Edgar Froese playing the fattest layered synthesizer sounds you can imagine. Everything about this album makes you want to dance. This disc was definitly made to be played in a club or at a party. The heavy backbeat and clean melodies are very infectious. What I like about this album as opposed to other dance tunes is that the songs don't get boring. However, the songs are not so bizarre that you can't follow along. Different sounds are introduced to keep things fresh. My two sons want to take this disc to the roller rink and skate to something other than the usual rap. \"The Dream Mixes\" is a must-have for anyone that likes to move or needs to be inspired into action. \"And Not Only That\" has an incredible mix of instruments including saxophone, trombone, trumpet, piano, guitar, vibes, and bass. The list of personnel has over twenty people in various combinations. The only thing consistent between each song is that Tegler is playing the drums. The strange thing is that the credits show everyone playing on the last song \"And Not Only That.\" But how does is sound? Brooks Tegler is a good drummer that doesn't fill every song with a solo. In fact, I was surprised at how few drum solos there were. The solos that are found are thoughtfully placed and punctuate the song rather than making it a bore. I liked the collection of original material and timeless Ellington numbers. The guitar and sax were excellent on the classic \"Lullaby Of Birdland.\" The band as a whole was very good with a solid rhythm section. The title cut is a fusion of rockabilly guitars and big band horns. Most of the songs were instrumental but the vocals somehow reminded me of Nat Cole. If I had to use one word to describe this disc, I would use smooth."
    ],
