    "body": [
      "One of my all-time favorite discs is \"Son Of Sam I Am\" by Too Much Joy. After that release, they started to take their act a little too seriously and went downhill. Luckily, here comes The Wynona Riders to take their place in my lineup of satirical punkified pop, and the sound similarity is uncanny. There's obviously no need to expound upon the band name, except to point out the rather lengthy tongue-in-cheek attempt in the liner notes to explain how the name has absolutely nothing to do with the actress. The CD cover art is a take off on everyone's favorite banned book, Salinger's 'Catcher In The Rye', right down to the maroon and gold background and lettering. The disc itself is full of aggressive guitar tracks, interspersed with a wacky assortment of filler including a few short (six to 43 second) piano fumblings, the Fat Albert theme song (a personal fave), a take off on the Power Rangers theme (the most requested song in my house right now by the neighborhood kids - they're too busy dancing to listen to the lyrics), and the Juicy Fruit TV commercial jingle. If the Sex Pistols were still playing today, assuming they would have learned to play their instruments by now, they would probably sound a lot like the music here. There's a controlled chaos to most of the songs, with a sarcastic approach to the whole thing. With the exception of the techno \"The Bogus Track\", all the songs are based on frenzied guitar tracks and screaming vocals. The covers of \"Kids In America\" (Kim Wilde) and \"The Great Rock n Roll Swindle\" (Sex Pistols) are outstanding. Once again, Edgar Winter is out to prove there's more to the legend than \"Frankenstein\" and \"Free Ride\" as he gathers up support from fellow veterans such as Jeff \"Skunk\" Baxter, Rick Derringer, Jermaine Jackson, Ronnie Montrose, Leon Russell, brother Johnny, and the infamous White Trash horns for another go in a career that has spanned over twenty years, and just as many albums. This one is a sweeping journey through the many styles he has become known for over the years, with the horns featured in the opening four cuts of R&B flavored rock, the third of which - \"We Can Win\" - was co-written with Russell and leans heavily on the B of R&B. \"Give Me The Will\" is next, with an old time rock ballad feel, highlighted by nice keyboard and vocal harmonies. After a couple of good rockers, Winter shows he can do the ballad with the best of them in \"Sanctuary\", a beautiful and soothing standout. \"The Music Is You\", written for his wife, is another shot at a ballad, but Winter's vocals aren't as strong as they are in \"Sanctuary\", and this one, except for the lyrics, falls a little short. The closer - \"What Do I Tell My Heart\" - comes out of left field, or the lounge, if you will, with it's smokey jazz feel led by piano and horns. Surprisingly, the vocals here are right on target, and I have to add this one to the top cuts list. I'll be honest - when this one came in, I kind of chuckled and put it off as the latest dino-rocker trying yet another comeback. What a pleasant surprise it turned out to be! Do you like bass? I mean REALLY like bass? Victor Wooten has taken a bold step and released an album of solo bass, and at times you'll find it hard to believe there's not more going on behind him. As a founding member of Bela Fleck And The Flecktones, Wooten has received many accolades, including \"Most Inspirational Bassist Of The Year\" from Bass Player magazine, and \"Best Jazz Bassist\" from Guitar Player. On his first solo release, he steps into the spotlight and delivers, from the funky rhythms of \"U Can't Hold No Groove\", the jazz or new age peacefulness of \"The Vision\" and Stevie Wonder's \"Overjoyed\", and the maniacal picking in the title cut and \"Classical Thump\". There is an occasional vocal dub, but this primarily an instrumental showcase of Wooten's range of playing styles, and one that will no doubt surprise some of those who have only thought of the bass as a nice supporting instrument."
    ],
