      "Steve Peer, a Maine director of special education for local schools, has assembled a vast assortment of musical talent from his area into a movie soundtrack. Strange thing though, there is no movie (yet). Taking a cue from recent success of other movie soundtrack compilations, it seemed the logical thing to do. Peer has traded in his days of spending weekend nights playing in his own bands, to working on his upstart record label. This compilation is the second release of local Maine music hopefuls on Peer's label. The bands represented here help defray costs of producing the discs. As I mentioned, there is a VERY wide variety of music on this disc, with at least one or two to please everyone. So in typical CDReviews.com compilation style, here's a track-by-track rundown. Homer Frake - Unwind The Crinkle A cross between The Residents or Fred Frith and raw New Orleans street blues. E.K.S. Crew - Desert Isle A great free-form jazz tune with incredible piano work. Zara - No Old Candy, Going To 9 A spacey, spoken word or light rap set to the beatings of a tribal drum./dd> Kathleen McGraw - Friendship A lyrically-challenged accapella ditty, with a twinge of Brooklyn in her voice. Gary Bosk - The Only Love You Have A lo-fi acoustic folk-blues cut, with an eerie Jethro Tull sound from the Stand Up days. Chidy Ho - Black Clouds ice country/adult crossover sound, ala Mary Chapin Carpenter or Kathy Mattea. Derrick Semler - What Can I Do? The Everly Brothers meet Roy Orbison and play an outtake from a 60's Byrds album. Soda - Chip Old Nick Lowe, down to the vocals, or Stiff classic sound. Tina Flagg - Dark Ride A definate 60's sound, with a Bananarama fluff. Frank Gotwals - Dry Ice Acoustic guitar and vocals, folksy, nice sound, better voice. Neil Davis 4 - Nowhere To Hide The first real rocker on the disc, there's a Who influence in this group somewhere. Reach - Mountain The Allmans music graced with the voice of Jerry Garcia. Crampton - Mannequin A haunting acoustic, with nice guitar work, that would fit in on either the Tommy (Who's version) or Jesus Christ Superstar (original) soundtrack. Cooper Hill Band - Let's Go See The Pope A bluesy, offbeat harmonica with a female Jim Carroll (Basketball Diaries) touch. Chi-Gong And The Nuclear Sioux - The Bomb A 60's or 70's San Francisco acid sound. Cotten Mather Olmstead - Bye, Bye Baby Goodbye A chaotic mishmash of They Might Be Giants crashing into Talking Heads. Paul Anderson And Lei Garofalo - Carry That Rock A folky, churchy acoustic sound (is this a cover?) with almost a kids song feel. Elgin Marbles And The Betrayers - Up All Night Is it blues, or is it jazz? Almost a big band beat. Whatever you want to call it, it's catchy. Live Band - When The Botmon Comes A girlish alternapunk tune. A cross between Lene Lovich, the Go-Go's, and Cyndi Lauper. Gangster - Gloria The classic redone, bluesed up. Killer! Night Kitchen - Just Kidding Potentially the nicest cut on the disc, an acoustic, sit back and just listen song. Too bad the recording quality is less than good. Kaleidoscope - I Will Have You A moaning, muted, Enya vocal track set to psychedelic 60's acid guitar. As I mentioned, there is a VERY wide variety of music on this disc, with at least one or two to please everyone. So in typical CDReviews.com compilation style, here's a track-by-track rundown. Homer Frake - Unwind The Crinkle A cross between The Residents or Fred Frith and raw New Orleans street blues. E.K.S. Crew - Desert Isle A great free-form jazz tune with incredible piano work. Zara - No Old Candy, Going To 9 A spacey, spoken word or light rap set to the beatings of a tribal drum./dd> Kathleen McGraw - Friendship A lyrically-challenged accapella ditty, with a twinge of Brooklyn in her voice. Gary Bosk - The Only Love You Have A lo-fi acoustic folk-blues cut, with an eerie Jethro Tull sound from the Stand Up days. Chidy Ho - Black Clouds ice country/adult crossover sound, ala Mary Chapin Carpenter or Kathy Mattea. Derrick Semler - What Can I Do? The Everly Brothers meet Roy Orbison and play an outtake from a 60's Byrds album. Soda - Chip Old Nick Lowe, down to the vocals, or Stiff classic sound. Tina Flagg - Dark Ride A definate 60's sound, with a Bananarama fluff. Frank Gotwals - Dry Ice Acoustic guitar and vocals, folksy, nice sound, better voice. Neil Davis 4 - Nowhere To Hide The first real rocker on the disc, there's a Who influence in this group somewhere. Reach - Mountain The Allmans music graced with the voice of Jerry Garcia. Crampton - Mannequin A haunting acoustic, with nice guitar work, that would fit in on either the Tommy (Who's version) or Jesus Christ Superstar (original) soundtrack. Cooper Hill Band - Let's Go See The Pope A bluesy, offbeat harmonica with a female Jim Carroll (Basketball Diaries) touch. Chi-Gong And The Nuclear Sioux - The Bomb A 60's or 70's San Francisco acid sound. Cotten Mather Olmstead - Bye, Bye Baby Goodbye A chaotic mishmash of They Might Be Giants crashing into Talking Heads. Paul Anderson And Lei Garofalo - Carry That Rock A folky, churchy acoustic sound (is this a cover?) with almost a kids song feel. Elgin Marbles And The Betrayers - Up All Night Is it blues, or is it jazz? Almost a big band beat. Whatever you want to call it, it's catchy. Live Band - When The Botmon Comes A girlish alternapunk tune. A cross between Lene Lovich, the Go-Go's, and Cyndi Lauper. Gangster - Gloria The classic redone, bluesed up. Killer! Night Kitchen - Just Kidding Potentially the nicest cut on the disc, an acoustic, sit back and just listen song. Too bad the recording quality is less than good. Kaleidoscope - I Will Have You A moaning, muted, Enya vocal track set to psychedelic 60's acid guitar. Expect the unexpected. With a name like The Young Dubliners, you'd think this would be an Irish-folky kind of disc. Not totally wrong, but you could pick just about any music category and be able to find a song with at least a trace. Dublin natives Keith Roberts (vocals, acoustic guitar) and Paul O'Toole (vocals, guitar, mandolin, banjo, harmonica) started the band in 1988 as an acoustic duo playing pubs in Los Angeles. Over the years, they added Randy Woolford (electric guitar), Dublin native Bren Holmes (bass), Jeff Dellisanti (sax, keyboards), Jon Mattox (drums, percussion), and Chas Waltz (violin, vocals) and discovered electricity along the way. True to their Celtic roots, \"Breathe\" contains two traditional Irish tunes - a ferocious rendition of \"Follow Me Up To Carlow\" and \"Foggy Dew\". For the rest of the disc, the band retains the Celtic sound, but to a much lesser extent, choosing instead to adopt a more Americanized rock approach. The one track that stands out is the Hal Ketchum sounding \"Mary\", a mellow look at a 16-year-old who leaves home for the wild life, has a child, gets abused and left, and decides she's had enough of life. The nice instrumental work and strong vocals leave this one with \"hit single\" written all over it. The Young Dubliners don't pigeonhole their sound, and provide lots of twists and turns. \"Wash My Hands\" and \"Shake Me Down\" are Spin Doctor type rockers, the former containing a get-your-attention guitar solo midway through. \"Thunder\" has a slower, almost classic-rock sound that I find enjoyable, even though I'm not exactly a fan of that music genre. \"Change The World\" is a catchy, harmonica filled good time melody, perfectly suited for an outdoor beer drinking dance party. \"Shame\" is another along those lines, with a strong Celtic flavor that deserves to be chased with an ice cold Harp. \"Don't You Worry\" is another slower cut, but seems to be missing something. This is perhaps the only time the band fails to shine. With a more aggressive approach here than their 1994 debut \"Rocky Road\", The Young Dubliners are heading in the right direction. This is a good listen, and an especially good addition to your rowdy party rotation."
