      "\"Deep\" could either be referring to the record company or the depth of talent found on this compilation release of some of the South's best unsigned bands. Some of the bands on display here have product of their own available, but many of the cuts here are unavailable anywhere else. Some of the bands here are familiar ones to those with an ear to the indie scene - Big Ass Truck and Dayroom who have reviews online here at IMRS - as well as All Mighty Senators and Uncle Mingo to name a few. Here's a quickie rundown of what's included: Stillwater - Rootsy pop, with a heavy guitar accent. A boring, repetitive song. Big Ass Truck - Funky sound with a psychodelic organ kicker, one of the more imaginitive acts here. 9811 - The blues rock of Black Crowes, with a hint of Dave Matthews style pop thrown in. Easily the first you'll be singing along with. Dayroom - Leon Redbone smashing into early Talking Heads at 75 mph. Fun and a good follow up to 9811. Your feet are still moving, keep it up. Mary Sunshine - Disappointing homogonized crossbreed of Live and Pumpkins. The Floyds - Rest time is over now, as the band formerly known as Floyd's Funk Revival flashes back to Sly Stone or Mothers Finest at their funkiest and rockingest all in one. Marcy Playground - Someone called this \"Nirvana light\", and for the first couple seconds, I was almost agreeing. I'd have to upgrade that Nirvana reference to possibly Lenny Kravitz. A little bit o' soul, a little bit o' blues, a little bit o' rock. Jeff Carroll And The Desmonds - A somewhat bland mix of 70's rock and 90's power pop. Athenaeum - Early 90's college sound, spacey pop jangles. All Mighty Senators - Funk, lightcore rap somewhere between George Clinton and Cypress Hill. Uncle Mingo - Shiny happy pop that even your mother will like. The Reejers - Does anyone remember the artsy rock from the 70's - Yes, Floyd, ELP, Parsons - here's a rehash. Wears old after a couple minutes. Donna The Buffalo - Cheesey sounding synth reggae. Barely Pink - \"I hope you don't resent this, I've never been to Memphis, but I think I love Elvis more than you.\" Great line, great pop song. Buck - Jerky Boys or Howard Stern phone call comedy skit. How many times can you listen to this? Check out this deal from Hardknocks - seven compilation discs for ten bucks. Jeez, you can probably find at least one you like and trade in the rest for a profit! This is one of the set (a complete list is at the end of this file with links to other reviews). The bands here are from Wisconsin, Ohio, and parts of Canada. Following our normal compilation process, here's a rundown of the bands and music featured on this set. Stronghold Classic crash pop or commercial punk, whichever way you want to look at it. A mid point of Husker Du meets Green Day. The first four of their songs here are pretty good, but \"Utilise\" is awful, thankfully it's less than a minute long. The High Plains Drifters Any band whose singer is named Ramone is alright with me. There's too much rock here to be punk, too much punk to be rock. Classic 80's midwestern US snot rock ala Replacements or Soul Asylum, with a shit kicking sound. My favorite on this particular disc. Mercurys Traditional low-budget, low-fi guitar. We all had a friend who played in a local band growing up. We knew they weren't going anywhere, but it sure was fun going to see them play. That's what I hear with this band. Lots of energy, probably real loud in the club, but nothing that hasn't been done thousands of times before. The Losers Cramps and Stranglers type punk from the 80's, and I used to love this stuff. It still sounds pretty good to me, although I'm not sure there's a market anymore for this sound. Oswald's Walking Connection High energy crash pop, again with a definiate early 80's punk sound, and with a little more production work, could latch onto to the resurrection of so-called punk with bands like Green Day, Rancid, and Goldfinger. Disfigurenes Yet another 80's sounding band, Go-Go's crossed with Joan Jett. Lots of uh-oh-oh's mixed in with a heavy guitar base. This disc is starting to turn into a K-tel classic 80's sounding release. That's not necessarily a bad thing. The Infected Good old American hardcore with lots of angst. Everyone is a target, and with lyrics like \"die yuppie scum\" and \"what happened to your dong, it used to be quite long\", how can you argue? Stillwater - Rootsy pop, with a heavy guitar accent. A boring, repetitive song. Big Ass Truck - Funky sound with a psychodelic organ kicker, one of the more imaginitive acts here. 9811 - The blues rock of Black Crowes, with a hint of Dave Matthews style pop thrown in. Easily the first you'll be singing along with. Dayroom - Leon Redbone smashing into early Talking Heads at 75 mph. Fun and a good follow up to 9811. Your feet are still moving, keep it up. Mary Sunshine - Disappointing homogonized crossbreed of Live and Pumpkins. The Floyds - Rest time is over now, as the band formerly known as Floyd's Funk Revival flashes back to Sly Stone or Mothers Finest at their funkiest and rockingest all in one. Marcy Playground - Someone called this \"Nirvana light\", and for the first couple seconds, I was almost agreeing. I'd have to upgrade that Nirvana reference to possibly Lenny Kravitz. A little bit o' soul, a little bit o' blues, a little bit o' rock. Jeff Carroll And The Desmonds - A somewhat bland mix of 70's rock and 90's power pop. Athenaeum - Early 90's college sound, spacey pop jangles. All Mighty Senators - Funk, lightcore rap somewhere between George Clinton and Cypress Hill. Uncle Mingo - Shiny happy pop that even your mother will like. The Reejers - Does anyone remember the artsy rock from the 70's - Yes, Floyd, ELP, Parsons - here's a rehash. Wears old after a couple minutes. Donna The Buffalo - Cheesey sounding synth reggae. Barely Pink - \"I hope you don't resent this, I've never been to Memphis, but I think I love Elvis more than you.\" Great line, great pop song. Buck - Jerky Boys or Howard Stern phone call comedy skit. How many times can you listen to this?"
