    "body": [
      "Hardknocks records has come up with not only an inexpensive way for Wisconsin bands to get exposure, but for music lovers everywhere else to get exposed to some varied new sounds. These compilations are available for a reasonable price ($5 USA, $6 Canada, $9 elsewhere, including postage), and feature over 60 minutes each of Wisconsin's finest. Affy Bangster Affy Bangster is a nice mixture of jangly guitars and pop ditties that would fit in perfectly at your average college radio station. They have a raw garage sound similiar to early Replacements. Onslaught Guitar metal, lots of guitars. Salzman can do damage with the guitars, but production quality is somewhat lacking at times. Arc Of Descent Some cleaner production on this metal band, but they're hurt a little by the vocals, which sound as if Martin is in the middle of a root canal with no medication. Mr. Nice Guy Awesome guitar work on \"Quick Fix\", a short little instrumental that definately gets your attention from the start. Decent production, and a sound that resembles Poison, or very early Motley Crue (pre-major label). The kind of music that says, \"hey, we're having fun and if you want to join in, fine, if not, well, that's fine too\". \"Will Will Robinson\" is worth the price of the disc alone. Cyanosis Noise metal, does absolutely nothing positive for me. Sorry. Boyd Rocket Garage punkish, popish, early Talking Heads minus the Byrne wizardry. It's always nice to see those in a position to offer something to the community actually doing it, and here is another of those with a slightly different twist. The thirteen tracks here are a result of songwriting workshops organized by In Harmony With The Homeless with graduates of The Los Angeles Mission rehabilitation program working with award winning singers and songwriters to spread a positive message of optimism, hope, and survival. Some of the artists represented here are Jonathan Cain (Journey), Marty Raybon (Shenandoah), Richie Havens and Rita Coolidge. Other contributions include the bluesy strains of David Morgan, the sensual Tori Amos impression by Laura Harding, the Jersey shore roughness of Dan Bern, and the R&B of Mavis Staples. With proceeds from sales going to homeless projects across the country, including the Los Angeles Mission, as well as royalties for those former homeless who have battled back and contributed to the songwriting here, this is your chance to help. On top of the satisfaction you'll no doubt feel, you also get a mighty fine collection of music. Consider this a soundtrack to the book, an audio history of the music that defined the Memphis blues scene, and that keeps it going strong today. The book's author, Robert Gordon, compiled the songs here, and contributed a nice textual companion in the liner notes that puts a lot of reasoning behind the selections. With homage to Dewey Phillips, the DJ who brough a lot of this music to the ears of unsuspecting listeners in the 50's, this disc spans the blues era from the single string guitar and footstomping of Moses Williams, to the contemporary sounds of current local faves Big Ass Truck. As if to illustrate the heritage behind the Memphis music scene, included here is the vocal solo of \"Boll Weevil\" by Sid Selvidge, whose son Steve is the guitarist for Big Ass Truck. These songs may not be chart classics, but in the spirit of the music, one might say they helped define the course of music for years to come."
    ],
