      "\"Sticks And Stones\" is the second of two indie compilations from Monkeyland Records (see also the IMRS review for \"One Nation, Underground\" ), featuring various acts from around the country. Many different types of music are featured here, and for $9ppd, it's a great way for you to experience the so called undiscovered bands making music and trying to catch the ears of the major labels. This one is a little bland as compared with the first, so if you have to choose one, go back to the other. As with all compilation reviews here, we'll take it one track at a time, with a little note about each one. White Out - Surf Star Is Dead (3:37) A great combination of the sound of the old 415 label (Wire Train/Translator/Red Rockers), blues rock, and surf guitars. A catchy guitar based rocker. Nervewomb - Unfinished (3:51) Can't figure out if this is an attempt at metal or hardcore. Either way it doesn't work. Heather Didn't Call - Advantage (3:05) Not a bad mix of light punk, serious drums, and lots of mass vocals - Green Day less the slick production. The Meerkats - The Room (4:20) Memories of many mid 80's bands spring forth on this one, The Church and The Call, early gothic kind of guitar sound. Vocals are lacking at times, sounding kind of bored with the whole thing. Indian Rope Burn - Crying Muscle (4:46) As I mentioned in the review of \"One Nation, Underground\", I'm no great fan of ambient, but this one is a decent mix of ambient and R&B, with some great screams. The male talkovers are something that I can do without, but at least the music is more than just test patterns. American Standard - Get It Right (4:51) Something is missing, but I'm not sure exactly what it is. There are hints of Collective Soul here, even some Beatles White Album era. Very 90's though, ready for MTV, whatever that's worth. Soul Driver - Blind (2:45) This one reminds me too much of the 70's space sound, groups like Yes or Jefferson Airplane (not even Starship). Not my cup of tea, so to speak, but if it's yours, here's another. Musically and vocally, it's solid. Drip - Helva (5:01) Whoa. Another of those \"what do I want to be\" songs. There are some neat pop jangles, then some metal-rap, some death growls, some hardcore guitar. Pick one then come back and see me. Real Cool Rain - Memories Pour Down (2:24) A nice mellow sound, musically comparible to some of the old Smiths stuff. However, as with their song on One Nation, Underground, the singer is snoozing. Perk him up a bit, this would be a great one with a little more energy in the vocals. Icarus - Icarus Descent (4:26) When did the Go-Go's get back together? Very Kim Wilde-ish early 80's pop. Ten years too late, but decent nonetheless. World In A Room - What's To Change (3:26) My fave, without a doubt. I've been waiting for one to jump out and grab my attention, and I finally have it. A mix of Smiths, ABC, and Fat Larry's Band (does anyone else know about this one). All that was good about the 80's is recaptured here. Cadaver Dogs - Sybil Buck (1:57) Your basic, no-frills, three-chord, play it fast, garage band. Singing a love song, no less. Kind of grows on you after a while if you're into that type of music. Shane McGregor - Give Back My Heart (4:11) A mellow, almost A/C kind of song. Potential abounds, but for the VH1 kind of audience. Night Shade - Larry (2:36) More techno. Is there something that makes one techno/ambient song better than the next? Luckily it's short. Little Savage - Choices (6:15) Light metal, but too long. Has the obligatory screaming guitar solo, but not much else worth mentioning. Betty Stress - Oh Boy (3:06) Here's what I said about their selection on the \"One Nation\" disc: One of the least impressive selections here. High school metal, poor vocals, blah blah blah... Same goes here. Reflecting Skin - Lifestyles Of The Non-Existent (5:08) I like the name. Theme music for your local halloween party or the next Freddy movie. Tides Of Despair - Endeavors (4:18) A guitar based instrumental desparately seeking vocals to give it strength. It's ok, but would be better shortened into a mid- song break. Transistor - Noncommittal (2:53) Why? Not a bad 18-song compilation. This one would better serve the overall purpose by being left off the disc. Sounds like some drunk high school kid recording vocals on a cheesy tape deck over someone else's instrumental track. I did that once, but I was in high school, and drunk, and, oh nevermind... \"One Nation, Underground\" is the first of two indie compilations from Monkeyland Records (see also the IMRS review for \"Sticks And Stones\" ), featuring various acts from around the country. Many different types of music are featured here, and for $9ppd, it's a great way for you to experience the so called undiscovered bands making music and trying to catch the ears of the major labels. As with all compilation reviews here , we'll take it one track at a time, with a little note about each one. The Grovers - Big, Black, And Round (4:00) A sound VERY similiar to old Talking Heads, right down to the \"I-eye-eye-eye-I\" and \"hey hey hey\" chants. Maybe a little harder edge to it with the guitars, but if I didn't know. And lines like \"My God, my goodness. Heavens to Betsy, holy cow\" sound like vintage Byrne. Chrome-Moly Violets - Highschool Yearbook (4:12) I like this one the best. Vocals like old Wreckless Eric or Paul Westerberg with a poppy Replacements beat. World In A Room - Train (5:07) Dark, gothic feel, ala old Church or Wall Of Voodoo. Not too bad really, and gets better after a few listens. Cupid Garden - Mind Fly (3:56) Run of the mill shoe-gazer bore. There's a reason this music fad didn't last longer than it did. Oh, you blinked? Sor - 244 (3:35) A little louder and a little faster than all of the previous. Could be described as light metal meets light punk, but not anything spectacular. Zen Parade - Broadcast Day (4:00) A throwback to the late 70's/early 80's british new wave, very comparible to old (OLD) Spandau Ballet, Duran Duran, or Ultravox. I have a special place in my heart for that era, so I give this one good marks. The Krayons - Failing Miserably (3:23) > Another high-energy guitar, along the lines of Too Much Joy, but without their catchiness. Very typical indie, and bound to stay that way. Sandrew - Pas De Bourree (3:54) Nice instrumental featuring beautiful acoustic guitar. A big change from the rest of the selections here, and very welcome. I'm not sure if it fits comfortably into the new age classification, but I'd sure like to hear more of it! Romeo Plowboy - Where Are You (3:47) Mid 80's danceable new wavish. There was a song back then called \"Somebody's Watching Me\" that occasionally pops up on that MTV/VH1 flashback hour. Everytime I listen to this song, it immediately reminds me of that other one. Neither, for me, are worth remembering, but if you're into that era of music, this one fits the bill perfectly. Real Cool Rain - Greener (4:43) Musically not bad, but someone give the singer a cup of coffee or a Jolt Cola. There's an instrumental break halfway through, and I think it's because the singer fell asleep at that point. Klump - Tommy (3:25) Here's one you're either going to love or hate - no inbetween. Very mid-80's pop influenced, Phil Seymour/Producers kind of stuff. Little Savage - Multi-Personality (5:21) This is another out of place selection, but at the other end of the spectrum from Sandrew. More guitars, but of the heavy metal type, and very generic. Nothing here that gets me excited, but if you're into metal, this sounds just like every other metal band you've ever heard. Poop - My Room (3:51) Great name. Not what I'd expect from a band named Poop though. Somehow hardcore came to mind, but this is definitely not, nor is it Poop. This is another with similarities to Talking Heads, even if the majority of the guitar work sounds like something from a cheap Bananarama dance track. The song changes about half way through to a psychedelic sort of guitar instrumental. Betty Stress - The Joke (3:51) One of the least impressive selections here. High school metal, poor vocals, blah blah blah... Night Shade - 1st Week Of Winter (3:37) Am I the only one who believes all ambient/techno sounds the same? Test patterns from someone's old casio electronic keyboard. The Candy Snatchers - Lil' Lolita (1:26) A fun throwback to the early whiny punk days of the late 70's. Not much going for it these days, but fun if you still throw on that \"Never Mind The Bollocks\" platter from time to time. White Out - Surf Star Is Dead (3:37) A great combination of the sound of the old 415 label (Wire Train/Translator/Red Rockers), blues rock, and surf guitars. A catchy guitar based rocker. Nervewomb - Unfinished (3:51) Can't figure out if this is an attempt at metal or hardcore. Either way it doesn't work. Heather Didn't Call - Advantage (3:05) Not a bad mix of light punk, serious drums, and lots of mass vocals - Green Day less the slick production. The Meerkats - The Room (4:20) Memories of many mid 80's bands spring forth on this one, The Church and The Call, early gothic kind of guitar sound. Vocals are lacking at times, sounding kind of bored with the whole thing. Indian Rope Burn - Crying Muscle (4:46) As I mentioned in the review of \"One Nation, Underground\", I'm no great fan of ambient, but this one is a decent mix of ambient and R&B, with some great screams. The male talkovers are something that I can do without, but at least the music is more than just test patterns. American Standard - Get It Right (4:51) Something is missing, but I'm not sure exactly what it is. There are hints of Collective Soul here, even some Beatles White Album era. Very 90's though, ready for MTV, whatever that's worth. Soul Driver - Blind (2:45) This one reminds me too much of the 70's space sound, groups like Yes or Jefferson Airplane (not even Starship). Not my cup of tea, so to speak, but if it's yours, here's another. Musically and vocally, it's solid. Drip - Helva (5:01) Whoa. Another of those \"what do I want to be\" songs. There are some neat pop jangles, then some metal-rap, some death growls, some hardcore guitar. Pick one then come back and see me. Real Cool Rain - Memories Pour Down (2:24) A nice mellow sound, musically comparible to some of the old Smiths stuff. However, as with their song on One Nation, Underground, the singer is snoozing. Perk him up a bit, this would be a great one with a little more energy in the vocals. Icarus - Icarus Descent (4:26) When did the Go-Go's get back together? Very Kim Wilde-ish early 80's pop. Ten years too late, but decent nonetheless. World In A Room - What's To Change (3:26) My fave, without a doubt. I've been waiting for one to jump out and grab my attention, and I finally have it. A mix of Smiths, ABC, and Fat Larry's Band (does anyone else know about this one). All that was good about the 80's is recaptured here. Cadaver Dogs - Sybil Buck (1:57) Your basic, no-frills, three-chord, play it fast, garage band. Singing a love song, no less. Kind of grows on you after a while if you're into that type of music. Shane McGregor - Give Back My Heart (4:11) A mellow, almost A/C kind of song. Potential abounds, but for the VH1 kind of audience. Night Shade - Larry (2:36) More techno. Is there something that makes one techno/ambient song better than the next? Luckily it's short. Little Savage - Choices (6:15) Light metal, but too long. Has the obligatory screaming guitar solo, but not much else worth mentioning. Betty Stress - Oh Boy (3:06) Here's what I said about their selection on the \"One Nation\" disc: One of the least impressive selections here. High school metal, poor vocals, blah blah blah... Same goes here. Reflecting Skin - Lifestyles Of The Non-Existent (5:08) I like the name. Theme music for your local halloween party or the next Freddy movie. Tides Of Despair - Endeavors (4:18) A guitar based instrumental desparately seeking vocals to give it strength. It's ok, but would be better shortened into a mid- song break. Transistor - Noncommittal (2:53) Why? Not a bad 18-song compilation. This one would better serve the overall purpose by being left off the disc. Sounds like some drunk high school kid recording vocals on a cheesy tape deck over someone else's instrumental track. I did that once, but I was in high school, and drunk, and, oh nevermind..."
