    "body": [
      "If ever there was a band waiting to do the Waffle House commercial jingle, SCOTS is the one. Short order trashabilly, with a side order of grits. You can get some background info from the Dirt Track Date review, let's get to the music. Where Dirt Track Date left off, this one picks right up, but with a little less sizzle. It seems there was more thought put into this one, and when you rely on a devil-be-damned attitude, that can sometimes be a curse. In this case it doesn't hurt too much, but for first timers to SCOTS, I'd recommend Dirt Track Date as a starting point. On this one, the band gets off to a fine start, with \"Shotgun\" along the same rockin' lines as \"Soul City\", about the southern traditional shotgun wedding (\"ya know boy, ya better run\") and \"Earthmover\" following with a true story of suburban sprawl. \"Banana Puddin'\" is classic SCOTS, paying homage to their southern roots \"somethin' funky with the skin on top, it's day old and bold, baby\". \"40 Miles To Vegas\", a true story of a hitchhikers nightmare, is a step off track for the band, with a harder rock sound. \"Love-A-Rama\" can't avoid the B-52's comparisions, with Mary Huff taking the lead on this big hair, shake your hips danceathon. The three instrumentals, \"Deja Varoom\", \"Theme From The Cheaters\", and \"Dance For Me\" work ok, but you kind of miss the off beat humor found in the lyrics of the others. \"Country Funk\" and \"Strangest Ways\" are lacking the traditional SCOTS punch, moving slowly and missing the get-up-and-move of most of the songs, although the latter does feature a nice surf guitar base. \"House Of Bamboo\" is an interesting cha-cha lounge boogie, complete with Huff on the Hammond. Less than great by SCOTS standards is still better than most, and if you need a quick pick-me-up, this one will do the trick. See Also: Dirt Track Date Oh no, not another 80's compilation! Wait . . . there's some pretty good stuff here, and no Toni Basil. How dare they pack this disc with 60 minutes of 80's music and not include Hey Mickey? Let's start with good taste. The selections here are concentrated on the early part of the decade, when college radio basked in the glow of synthpop and new wave, breaking in many of these bands before they moved from the left end of the dial into the clubs as dance floor staples. At the risk of dating myself, most of these saw heavy play on my shows during those years, eventually disappearing from my personal collection with the rest of the vinyl. And while most of these songs have shown up on a few other comps over the years, the filler hasn't been good enough for me to shell out the cash for one or two songs. Except for maybe two songs here (\"Pass The Dutchie\" by Musical Youth and \"I Wanna Be A Cowboy\" by Boys Don't Cry), this one has top notch selections from a memorable era. You've surely heard almost all of these songs at one point in your life, so there's no need going into details on the music. You'll be just as impressed by a list of those not mentioned so far: Everybody Have Fun Tonight (Wang Chung), Wild Wild West (Escape Club), Sex (Berlin), Love Plus One (Haircut 100), Our House (Madness), Hippychick (Soho), Perfect Way (Scritti Politti), I Ran (A Flock Of Seagulls), Saved By Zero (The Fixx). Now if only someone would include The Jags and Glass Moon on a compilation... Nothing fancy here, just your basic three chord, grind-em-up punk, packing 37 songs (plus an answering machine message) into 54 minutes. What separates this one from others like it (and knocks it up to a 4 rating from a 3) are the lyrics. If you believe all they tell you, you should feel sorry for these three from Minnesota. Nothing goes right for them, including losing local hockey teams, lots of girl problems, bad weather (back to back songs titled \"The Winter Sucks\" and \"Did I Mention The Winter Sucks\" with a second version of the first later on the disc), more girl problems, but through it all they manage to maintain a sense of humor. On \"I Still Miss My Ex\", the chorus continues \"...but my aim is improving. Sometimes I get real close but she keeps moving\". There's a break from the three chord slam in \"Keep Rock Alive\" with some great guitar riffs and a slight twinge of rockabilly work in \"Flannel\". Most of the songs lean a little towards crash pop, so so those of you seeking a more melodic punk sound should find this one acceptable. But it will appeal more to the grind core fans who appreciate their punk hard and to the point."
    ],
