    "body": [
      "We all know the internet is a great gathering place for people to meet, share ideas, and share tricks of the trade. If you're a group of musicians, those meetings sometimes turn into a compilation disc like this one put together by members of The Truth Hurts. Gathering groups from all over the world, they also managed to amass a musical medley of many different styles, smartly ordered on the disc so as not to interrupt the flow of the songs to a point of being distracting. First up is Greg Panfile's \"Shadow Woman\" is a nice spacey acoustic tune, but it's hurt by main lyrics that last only the first minute, then the chorus repeats for another two without much variation on the delivery. Toronto's The Truth Hurts are next with a couple that bring back memories of 80's synth pop ala Naked Eyes and Soft Cell, but with a bit more emphasis on the guitar. More of the same follows, minus the guitars, with another Toronto band, The Deal. Their song, \"Who's To Say\", is a very subdued, almost eerie sounding ballad that also owes an influence to the synth sounds of the 80's. Spin the globe for a couple from Australia's Ground Zero next. Their first selection, \"Nirvana's Edge\", features light keyboards and fuzzy guitar work, ending up sounding like a cross between The Lightning Seeds and The Moody Blues. \"Reality Fades\" is an acoustic guitar ballad, but is hurt by a female backing vocal that detracts from the otherwise nice song. I'll only mention that The Broken Group has a couple here that are strikingly out of place, with a distorted improvisational synth-jazz feel that I could have gone a long time without hearing. Also out of place is Australia's Bardling, with a cut that swerves out of control sounding a celtic-jazz-punk experiment gone bad. Another one from down under here is Prizm, with an interesting Stiff sounding funk cut, Ian Dury meets Captain Sensible if you would. Next is an interesting band from Wales named Box Of Frogs, with a harder sound than the others here, although the production on the cuts here is a little lacking. \"Stone Me\" has a decent guitar pop sound, and \"Namedropper\" could be a good hardcore cut if the guitars were produced at a little higher volume and the vocalist maintained his scream level accordingly. But is this the same Box Of Frogs that put out at least one album in the early-mid 80's that I bought on name alone? The intensity level continues to increase as Sweden's Lithium follow with the raucous rockers \"Flight 911\" and \"Warchild\", possessing great throbbing guitar work and a style similar to old Judas Priest. There is one more to close the disc, but I despised the whiney snotty female vocals, so I'll do the band a favor and leave their name off. There is some decent stuff here, especially the synth pop groups that make up the first half of the disc. To top it all off, this is an enhanced CD with some video clips and band backgrounders for your PC. Nowadays it seems everyone and their brother is in a ska band, and the flood of bands concentrated on such a distinct style makes it hard for one to stand out over another. When rating ska bands, I mainly look at four things - how is the horn section, will it stand up on the dance floor, is there enough variety to keep it from growing stale before the disc finishes, and do the vocals match the music. All the bases are covered here, the only knock being a little whine in vocalist John Arquilla's style. The horns are first rate for G-Spot, very well played, not overbearing, and with enough variation to make it through the disc without getting repetitious, credit on that last part to some great song-writing. As far as danceability, no problem whatsoever there - I dare you not to dance. I already mentioned the good songwriting, and the vocals, even with my knock above, are at least consistant and Arquilla's voice isn't bad, just a little annoying at first listen. Give it more than one spin and you'll forget the thought. Also worth mentioning is the great keyboard work on \"Organ Man\", and \"Bizarro\" mixing a Desi Arnez lounge Havana beat with some great old school jazz horns. A little salsa is required for the Mexican sound of \"Matador\", and the skip button is required for \"Ska Spot\", a bland instrumental overlaid with static voice distractions. This is one of those discs that didn't come with a whole lot of info, so I can't give you a decent background of the band. However, if you like your ska on the lighter-than-punk side, here's one to look at. Ready for some fun? The Piersons are here to provide just that with their second release, Appleberry Wine. Bassist Scott Moore provides the description \"too punk for pussies and too pussy for punks\" and we can stop right there. But we won't, adding that their sound is a raw garage-pop style, mixing in the best elements of The Replacements, old Rolling Stones, and the energy of personal power pop fave Greenberry Woods, or the offshoot Splitsville. Vocalist Patrick Sedillo (also on guitar) could easily pass for Paul Westerburg, and together with Moore and drummer Tony Chadwick, the band spits out a great collection of high energy, foot stomping rockers that would seem as home in a small smoke-filled bar as they would in a much larger venue. Overall, this is a full throttle assault, but you can try to categorize some of the songs into either power pop - \"Sometimes Sometimes\" with its frenzied guitar riffs, \"Just Like Now\", and \"Waitress\" - or Stones-ish blues pop - \"Lightnin' Speed\", \"California Eyes\" - or Replacements sound alikes - \"Got It Made\", \"Something Strange\" (w/Stones' guitar), and \"Insecurity\". One that defies these descriptions is \"Tease\", an odd inclusion in this collection with a groovy 60's sounds ala The Lovin' Spoonful's \"Do You Believe In Magic\". There is acoustic version of California Eyes as unlisted track 12 that is a nice soft finish to an otherwise raw pop experience. This one's a keeper, it should be for you too. Now I'm off to find their first release..."
    ],
