    "body": [
      "Think of this disc as an imaginary trip to the southwest US, where your entire trip is spent hopping from bar to bar, catching a new band every night. And not being familiar with the area and local talent, each stop is a new and exciting adventure. Save the airfare money, pick up a nice cold six pack, and drop this one in the player. The selection here is what you'd expect on this trip, a smattering of rootsy rock and country, a little twangy at times, but mostly somewhere comfortably in the middle. Starting the disc off is a rowdy rock/country (not country rock) cut from H.J. Robertson, backed by a driving drum beat supported by some mean harmonica. Although the chorus gets a little repetitive towards the end, it's a fine start for the variety to come. Next is Dan Israel And The Cultivators with one in the Americana/roots category, with vocals that evoke comparisons to early Dylan. Another in this vein is David Beaudry's \"Psychedelic Tom T. Hall\", complete with the whiney vocals that seem to define the commercially successful groups in this category, i.e. Jayhawks, Son Volt, etc. Next comes my favorite here, \"Fun And Games\" by Ignitors, a jangly guitar country/pop song that mixes the best of Jackson Browne and Radney Foster. Then we break a little from the pattern with a New York band, Stickman Jones, which is a little out of place here, almost like a New Yorker in the Southwest. It's a great song, with a style that you'd expect from Sarah MacLachlan or Mary Karlzen, and I especially like the mandolin. Our next stop is a good old fashioned honky tonk joint, with The Revenants as your host, making full use of the steel guitar with a sound very much like The Mavericks. Two more bands are playing at this place. Next up is Out Of The Blue with \"Heartbrake\", an old fashioned twangy Petticoat Junction type song. Not one of my personal faves, but well played nonetheless. Headlining this night are Grievous Angels, and this one features more of the old fashion country sound, but not as twangy as the last two. This one features the best guitar work of the collection. The Burnlackers offer up some more country, but aside from some nice gritty vocals from Sarah Temple, it's a middle of the pack type song. Get out the dance shoes for the next one, it's Jerry Lee Lewis meets The Mavericks in the person of Andy Tanas, and a fiddle hasn't rocked like this since Charlie Daniels enjoyed the spotlight. You need a rest after that last one, so Ronnie Glover slows it down with one that sounds like a young Johnny Cash doing a TV theme song. Hit the rest room now, there's still a few left. Stop at the bar and pick up another longneck while you're up. The next band, CAVU, is way out of place here and the screen may have to come down for their haunting, modern rock ballad sounding number that may send the empties flying. The last two will get you back on the dance floor. First Kenny Love And The Rockerfellas, and if you can imagine a young Elvis playing 90's country you have these guys. The disc closes much like it opened, with a fine country/pop piece from Norman Collins, drums leading the way, and a great guitar solo. I had a grand time on this trip, heard a lot of good music, but with every venture into the unknown, there are some potholes. If you navigate carefully, it's a ride worth taking. Wow! I'll try not to use too many superlatives, but this is just a phenomenal release, especially considering it is a debut, and the band never played live before recording. Is it for everyone? Probably not, but if you've ever owned a copy of Steely Dan's Aja, you need to add this one to your collection. It took pianist David Glassco almost nine months to piece together the band, and the time was well spent. The end result is a jazz/pop/soul masterpiece, performed and produced to perfection. Did I mention they sound an awful lot like Steely Dan? It doesn't take more than a second or two of the first cut, \"Under The Buzz\", to start those comparisons, and except for a couple brief diversions, they maintain that dubious sound throughout. If you listen closely to \"We Have A Right\", you can almost hear a reggae sound behind the horns. \"Complete\" is a little change, more of a rock beat based on the bass and drums, with some jangly guitar lingering in the background. \"Sarah's Scene\" is back to the jazz/pop, with a greater emphasis on the horns almost to the point of being an R&B cut. \"Annie\" is an interesting cut, alternating between smokey club jazz with the horns and a more uptempo dance friendly style. \"Satellites And Her\" is a nice mellow jazzy cut, but it has a chorus that repeats and repeats, and after a while gets a little tired. \"Mexico Sunset\" closes the disc with a step away from the rest, a southwestern serenade with the horns taking somewhat of a back seat and the nice guitar picking taking over the lead. I had a hard time keeping the top cuts list to my usual three or four because there's not a bad one in the bunch here. This might not be one I keep permanently in the player, but it will surely find a spot in my collection for those times when I don't want to rattle the walls. Part of the reason it gets such high marks is the overall quality, from the songwriting to the playing and the production. Did I mention they sound like Steely Dan? Top Cuts: Fun And Games (Ignitors), Simple (Stickman Jones), You Gotta Love Her (Andy Tanas), Loving Kind (Norman Collins)"
    ],
