    "body": [
      "Not much I can tell you about this one as far as background info goes, but I prefer to concentrate on the music anyway. This one is a hard one to classify, so for lack of a better term we'll use alterna-melodic-retro. Pretty vague, I know, but there are times on this disc where the band sounds like your typical 90's MTV modern rock band, bland and happy to fit in with the crowd. Then there are others where you'd swear Roger Daltry has discovered the fountain of youth, brought Keith Moon back from the dead, and put an old Who twist on the music of this decade to bring it back to life. The first half of the disc in particular has this dynamic Who sound, but there are lots of other flashbacks here. The guitars on \"Help Me\" are vintage Boy-era Edge, slashing through the song with the vigor long since lost to U2, and the closer \"O.N. (Ojos Negros) is a volume deprived addition, but moves with a drumbeat like something from U2's War era. \"Crawl\" starts with a Robert Plant outtake from Rock N' Roll, then keeps the pace slower than the others, with a nice touch on the tie-dye vocal distortions. The last few songs start to sound like filler, but there's no denying the potential here. Vocalist R. J. Vasquez has the power to dictate the band's success with his work, and there are a couple songs where he seems somewhat disinterested, and it hurts the end result. For the most part this is a pretty good release, and you can live with the few bummers when the rest is as good as it is. It has the much needed 90's generic alternative sound, plus that element of classic rock for us that other generation. This guy could be the most famous bluesman you've never heard of. Among the names who have joined Jimmy onstage include member of X, Fabulous Thunderbirds, and Los Lobos, as well as Ray Manzarek, Vince Neil, Wendy O'Williams (I would have paid big bucks to see that one), Belinda Carlisle, and Stevie Ray Vaughan. Jimmy has played shows with Springsteen, ZZTop, and Jackson Browne, and David Lee Roth immortalized Jimmy in Van Halen's 1984 song \"Top Jimmy\". On this disc, he's backed by members of Wingnut Supreme and Why?Things Burn, whose leader R. J. Vasquez handled production duties. If you like your blues polished and more in the R&B or rock/blues categories, stay away from this one. This is good old fashioned Albert Collins or Muddy Waters, got-no-money, my-dog-left-me, my-womans-no-good, lets-have-a-good-time-anyway blues, produced to perfection for the genre. All the edges are left unsmoothed, giving it a rough live sound that is so important when capturing a classic blues artist. There are a couple places where things start slacking off a little, such as the guitar solos in \"Slow Blue Something\", and the eerieness and dragging on of \"Voodoo Sausage\" which blows the continuity completely out of the water. \"Come On Home\" sounds more like a Wingnut Supreme song with Jimmy singing, instead of the blues that stoked so well for the first eight songs. Cut out the last couple songs and you've got some blues pure and hot, not lukewarm and watered down like too many of the wannabes. Here we have the first CD release from D.J. Lauria, featuring four songs that we reviewed nearly two years ago ( refer here ), and three new ones recorded just for this release. Lauria's songwriting style can be lumped in the power pop category, but is more pop than power. Looking at the three new ones, \"Huddleston Pond\" revives the Crenshaw comparison that appeared in that original review, lots of guitar, jangly as a backup, and powerful in the mid-song solo. \"Day Before We Met\" never builds to the tap-your-toes level of the others, and thus is a little disappointing among the others here. \"Emergency Stop\" is a 60-ish pop ditty, but where the backing vocals work nicely on \"Huddleston Park\", they would be better left off this one, coming up flat at times. \"Time That I Forgot\" is once again the best of the bunch, but two of the three new ones are right there with it. Last time I said I'd wait until I heard more from this Rhode Island trio before passing judgement, but Lauria is proving himself to be a solid songwriter."
    ],
