    "body": [
      "Before I get to the music, let me start this one with some number - 5, 7 and 14. As in five bucks, for seven bands and fourteen songs. That's a good deal right there, and as much as I love compilations, the price/quantity combination is certainly enticing. What about quality? Not too shabby either. The Faint starts this one off with a Cure/Shriekback sound. \"Some Incriminating Photographs\" has an eerie and quirky pop feel that relies on keyboard and drum basis with some guitar thrown in more as an attention getter. Their second selection, \"Typing: 1974-2048\" reminds me a lot of the old Cure song \"Grinding Halt\" from their pre-glam pop days. Cursive is next up with a more modern, heavy on guitar and feedback sound, but the singer sounds like he was in a lot of pain during the recording session, and the Bush-ish songs end up pretty bad as a result. Musically, good stuff though. Lullaby For The Working Class is an interesting act. \"Hypnotist (Song For Daniel H.) is a nice cut, even if does sound like it was recorded at a policeman's funeral with the bagpipes in the background. \"The Man vs. The Tide\" has a haunting quality, laid back in nature, but begging for your attention. Commander Venus falls into the same vein as Cursive, with good music, early Cure-ish sounding, but hurt a lot by poor vocals. Park Ave. has a familiar, 80's synth-pop-with-guitar feel, sort of Scandal sounding, but without the power of Patty Smythe to carry the band. Their first entry, \"Cloak And Dagger\", is the better of the two, better suited to the vocal range of the singer, who doesn't quite hit all the hit notes real well on their other selection. Gabardine is another that shows some promise here, with a quirky take on the generic 90's so-called alternative sound. A better production effort could clean these guys up into a radio ready outfit, probably more so than any of the others here. Bright Eyes is the finale here, with a couple lo-fi, analog four-track recordings that show off great writing skills. In reality, this is Conor Oberst, a 17 year-old who also appears in two of the bands above (Commander Venus and Park Ave), and is a name to remember in the future. As compilations go, this may not be one of the best, but it's certainly worth the price to hear some new music. Most of the songs have been amassed from 7\" releases, and the sound isn't always up to CD quality. But as with all compilations, you'll find some things to like here, and for five bucks, why not? No hidden punches thrown by this Vancouver based threesome, who have taken the Ramones two speed approach to music, fast and faster, added a Social D hardass melodic layer, and spit the results out directly in your face. Where most studio recordings tend to get carried away with the knobs and end up over produced leaving the listener wondering how the band would sound live, this one leaves no doubt that the band would be awesome live. Punk 'n' Roll is what they call it. Good, almost clean fun is what I'd use. With Brian Bresett on vocals and guitar, Greg Laikin on bass, and Dave Troutman on drums, this is a big sound from just three guys. The disc starts with a classic punk love song \"I know the real reason I'm pissed off all the time - I Miss You, I Miss You...\". On \"Hannah Don't Cry\", you can almost hear the Gabba Gabba Hey's as they ace their Ramones exam. Bresett steps out of the three chord smash style with some mean licks in \"Subterranean Holiday\" and you'll be tapping along to the punkabilly bass line in \"Bad Memory\". \"Tamed\" and \"Generation XS\" close the disc with two of the more punk sounding cuts, featuring hard drum lines and Bresett relying more on the scream approach than the others. I could have done without these two, and the unlisted track at the end with Bresett's distorted German 101 lesson words. For the first eight cuts, a great house rockin', annoy the neighbors kind of disc. I guess those last couple are when the cops get called. This Dallas band, led by Dave Christensen, relies on a well written, but somewhat lo-fi rock sound. Primarily a blues-rock band, they show their versatility by stepping outside those boundaries from time to time, usually with success. Listen closely to the majority of the songs and there's a hint of a stripped down old Stones sound. \"Time Will Tell\" and \"Still Believe\" have an alluring 60's mellow rock sound, and \"My Everything\" has the biggest Stones sound, especially in the guitar work. \"Tenderness\" is perhaps the best here, another of the mellow variety, with a nice rootsy Americana feel. \"You And I\" is another outside the norm, with a country pop feel, again ending up on the top cuts list. \"Change\" and \"In My Head\" are more funk than rock, but manage to squeeze a little of both into one song. \"One Of Those Nights\" has a loungy blues feel that works quite nicely. Let track 10 play through and you'll find a little reggae tune hidden at the end. This is one of those discs that benefited from a lot of listens. The first few times through, it didn't do a whole lot for me, but once the songs started settling in my head, they became much more likeable, especially the latter half of the disc. The band fares better on the slower stuff, and at times seems underpowered for the harder rock sound. Variety is key here, and there's plenty to choose from in that respect."
    ],
