    "body": [
      "When I write these things, I try not to read through the bio and promo material until I'm almost at the completed state, and then add any interesting fluff in at that time. That approach came back to haunt me a little on this one as my many references to Toad The Wet Sprocket had to be noted with the fact that that band's guitarist/vocalist, Glen Phillips, was actually part of this band on a revolving door basis for the recording of this disc. Musically, this disc is a bit odd. Not a bad odd, but sometimes not necessarily a good odd either. There are times when I absolutely love this one, and others when I, well, don't. I started with the Toad comparisons on the opening track, \"A Burning House\", which wasn't even written by Phillips, although I'm pretty sure he's handling vocals. \"Positively Double Negative\" is a light hearted attempt at funk-rock that doesn't fit with the rest of the disc too well. \"Calling Matt\" is an Andy Partridge type of pop song, and \"Lazy Susan\" tears a page out of the Sgt. Pepper era Beatles songbook. Next is one of those that'll get your finger searching for the next button on your player, a Phillips contribution titled \"Eye Wannabe Likes Lye\", which sounds more like Prince on quaaludes having a bad vocal day. Luckily it picks back up quickly with \"Doubly Doubting Thomas\", a heavy pop song ala Toad, and then another eerie age Beatles pop on \"The Frogs Are Alive\". Next is the gem of the disc, \"Sort This Out\", a quirky and disjointed pop song that will have you singing along by the second listen. \"Back To The Station\" turns up the speed a little with a heavier rock/pop sound, then \"Without\" closes the disc by dropping it back down with another with an eerie, mellow sound. What the electronics industry needs to come up with is a way for us to program the playing of a particular disc, then have the player recognize when that disc is inserted and recall the program selections. I'd have this one spend a lot of time in my player if it were possible to automatically skip a couple of these tracks. The ones that I like, I really like, and for that reason alone, it'll get a good rating on our scale, but I have to dock it at least one for those skippable cuts. Worth investigating if you're a modern pop, late era Beatles, or even mid-career XTC fan. And now for something a little different, from California comes this four piece who describe their sound as art-folk-pop, kind of a cross between Frente and Mary Karlzen. Vocalist Ellen Turner's delivery can be alluring and enjoyable one moment, but equally as annoying the next. Her style is more suited to the slower, acoustic material, which makes up most of the selections here, but there are a couple where she gets lost in the frenzy. The exception to this rule is \"Perpetual Thought\", one of the better cuts, where she shines on vocals even with the uptempo and electric backing. Top of the list is \"Maybe It's Time\", a guitar and vocal folk ballad, beautifully written and performed in its simplicity. \"Strong Medicine\" is one of the electric ones, a little on the artsy or eclectic side with its instrumentation (is that a word?), and should be one of the better cuts here, but Turner has a couple of moments where it sounds as if she's struggling with the pace again. Ditto for \"Desert Rain\" and \"Big Plans\". There are a couple of the odd variety in \"To The Three Musket Eaters\", \"Strange Land\", and \"Are Our Oars Out?\", that I could have done without hearing on this one, but the disc closes with a couple of nice melodic folkish tunes, \"Love Is Here To Stay\" and the eerie \"Death Is A Quality Of Life\". More good than bad here, but still one of those discs that will hard to listen to all the way through without skipping a song or two along the way. The band, especially Turner, shows strong promise of being acceptable to the masses, but they need to trim their song selection a bit before falling into that one and out category that plagued Frente and Karlzen when they hit the airwaves. I'm doing something I don't normally do here, and that's putting out a bad review. Normally, I'll hold off writing a bad one because I'd rather help the artist, and if that means not publishing a review, so be it. However, this one is a special case. Bright Eyes is 17 year-old Conor Oberst, also featured on the Saddle Creek Compilation , and although the quality of this recording leaves something to be desired, the songwriting does not. This is analog four-track, so it's pretty lo-fi, but if you pay attention to the songs themselves you'll find a style long past his 17 years, and a knack for writing songs that could easily be retrofitted into the rock/pop genre. Oberst also contributes to another couple bands, Commander Venus and Park Ave., both of which are also worth watching. This disc may not be worth remembering, but the artist is one you're bound to hear from again."
    ],
