    "body": [
      "I can't top their claims of \"Original Groove Rock\", but add in the old American Bandstand catchall - \"it's got a good beat, and you can dance to it\". This California band has a great style, mixing lots of elements into a friendly pop/rock sound that should cross a generation or two of listeners. The disc starts out with a couple INXS sounding rockers, lots of beat, lots of groove, but somehow ordinary. It gets better, as \"Again\" starts with a nice mellow folky sound, and picks up a little into a John Mellencamp/Van Morrison jangly rocker. \"Some Heroes\" tells a nice story of personal reform, live fast, die young, but touch a few souls before it's too late. Mostly a melodic ballad, sounding a little like Live, it does kind of get hurt by a misplaced electric solo towards the end. The groove is heavy on \"Moment Of Grace\", with some soulful background vocals and a killer bass solo. The top song here is \"Darwin Street\", another INXS type rocker that sounds like a can't miss for FM radio. I especially like the simplistic piano interlude which adds a nice touch. \"Willow Tree\" isn't one of the strongest cuts here, but it does solidify the sounds-like on the vocals. I've been referencing INXS, and whoever is on vocal duty does have a lot of Michael Hutchence in the sound, but there's also a lot of Garland Jeffries as well. The disc hits a little dry spell here in the middle, but \"Release Me\" picks things up a bit with an acoustic, harmonica laced, back porch kind of sing along, and \"Tahoe\" follows that with more of the same, but with a faster beat, certain to get you moving in one way or another. All in all, a pretty solid debut effort. This one should get added to all your party playlists, as it's guaranteed to bring about a festive mood. It's a good thing this Minneaplois foursome site XTC as one of their influences, because this disc sounds like another Dukes Of Stratosphere project. The six selections here could have been culled from the English Settlement outtakes, that period of XTC's illustrious career where they started the transformation from frantic rockers to cutesy pop star wannabes. There's a mix of both stages here, \"The Bridle\" and \"In Circles\" being the picks that best match the early days with the heavy emphasis on the guitar and less on the polish. \"Tragic Day\" and \"Double Bed\" are the cuts from the middle period, still lots of guitar, but a distinct move towards that radio friendly, albeit quirky, pop sound. \"Seize The Man Below\" is a fun song, a perfect fit for the late 80's/early 90's sound of XTC. The guitars are more jangly now, and a big push is evident towards a melodic sound, with more diverse instruments entering the mix. \"Plain\" can fit into all the phases, at times being a laid back ballad, others being a full fledged guitar rocker, almost with a metal feel, and surprisingly, the two styles meld together nicely. Sorry to be so focused on XTC with this one, but I've been a fan of theirs since the early days, and this one was almost like a retrospective look at their career. There have been a lot of changes over the years with Andy and the boys, and any fan of theirs, from any period, should take the time to investigate this one. Lots to like, but if you don't like to stray from the straight and narrow path of 90's modern/alternative/blahblah, perhaps this isn't the one for you. Someone needs to sign this Atlanta band and make them the stars they deserve to be. We reviewed their debut release, cleverly titled \" Debut Re-Release \" back in 1996, and it's one of the few from the old IMRS days that still gets regular play in my house. Originally that one got a 4 of 5 rating, and looking back at it now, it probably needs to be changed to a 5. Where that one left off, this one picks right up, easily making the hot picks list after only a couple listens. This one is a short, six song package, but still with plenty of time to show the versatility of the band. Opening the disc is the hardest of the six selections, \"I Do You\", sort of a confession of a love that isn't two way. While a good song, the best is yet to come. \"Hit Me\" is Leslie Gore meets Chrissie Hynde, an upbeat, yet depressing pop song about physical abuse. Vocalist Trish Thompson once again shines in \"All The Sin\", and combined with the jangly guitar backing, this one could be an old Pretenders song. Another highlight is \"Disaster Ever After\", a catchy pop beat, a little Loverboy wah wah guitar, more strong vocals, and before you know it your feet are tapping too. The haunting ballad \"Everchange\", a repeat from the last disc, closes this one with the reminder that not only can this band rock, but they can slow it down and not lose a beat, or your attention. Jump on this one now, so one day you can say you knew them when..."
    ],
