    "body": [
      "I'll get the obvious out of the way, that being Natalie Cortez sounds an awful lot like Natalie Merchant of 10,000 Maniacs fame. Musically, there are some similarities between the two, but for the most part, the comparison only holds to the vocals. The music here is far from a insomnia cure as much of 10,000 Maniacs turned out after a few listens. \"Ghost Of You\" has an Amy Ray/Indigo Girls feel, rooted in folk, but with more power than your average folk song. Except for a heavy dose of guitar in the middle and again towards the end, \"Everytime\" drags a little. \"I Ask\" is one of those that can be favorably compared to that other group, mainly because of the laid back rhythms, and the way Cortez accentuates certain syllables in her vocal delivery. The guitar work is a little heavier here, and the ending is a little odd, breaking into a guitar solo like you'd expect mid-song, then suddenly fading out as if they ran out of tape recording the jam. \"In The Nude\" is a catchy, more pop than folk song, with a little organ thrown in the mix. \"Three Times\" takes the pop approach again, with impressive, foot tapping results. Flashback to a semi-psychedelic 60's style ala Jefferson Airplane with \"The Dog\", a tried-but-true guitar rocker complete with pedal distorted guitar work. A more modern, rocking sound returns on \"Up All Night\", with Cortez giving her most impressive performance. \"Joey\" is the one where the band finally gets to turn it up a notch. You can feel it as the mellow start has a pulsing guitar beat just itching to break loose, and it does just that as the suspense builds into an all out jam. It's hard to come down after that one, and the segue into \"Tumble Me\" might be too big a shock, as this one is a nice acoustic folk ballad to close things out. There's an unlisted track about Henry Rollins at the end, but it's not worth saying much more. Cortez and company have a great sound for a band on their second independent release. The highs far outnumber the lows on this one, and if there's justice in the music business, this is another band you'll be hearing bigger and better things from in the future. I dare you to listen to this one and not be in a good mood within the first three songs. This first solo effort from This Living Hand co-founder is one worth a listen for followers of progressive power pop. Hubbard speaks of being able to experiment with these solo recordings, and the results are mostly of the successful variety. The candy coating of \"Paper Star\" with its double vocal track is irresistible, but the mix on \"Backshelf\" is a little too outrageous for the average pop fan. \"Captain Of The Summer\" is another great power pop song, but heavy on the keyboards and not the guitar, without being a generic synth-pop song. \"End Of Summer\" is probably my favorite here, with a very strong hint of Matthew Sweet in the vocals and guitar (although that can be said about a lot of the songs here), a light dose of keyboard to spice up the rhythm, and a little Beach Boys style vocals at the end. \"Innocence\" is a nice laid back pop ballad that is hurt a bit by overkill on the keyboard mix. \"Plastic Fight Song\" is an interesting closer, an odd mix of old Sweet and Peter Gabriel into a heavy drum and keyboard layered, roll the credits movie theme. There are hits and misses here. Sometimes Hubbard goes a little overboard on the keyboard, taking over a song that would have been better off with the emphasis somewhere else, but this is truly a fun album."
    ],
