    "body": [
      "Consider this disc a rollercoaster ride through the vague definition of modern day power pop, full of twists and turns, oddball insertions, and otherwise interesting diversions designed to avoid the rut of boredom. Led by writer/guitarist/vocalist Aaron Tap, this band operates at all speeds, sometimes slowing it down as they climb the hill, but always picking it up as you head back down and careen around the bends. The first part of the disc packs the best punch, with the prototype power pop of \"Buzz\", \"Waste\", and \"On The Outside\". They start to speed up a bit for the crash pop of \"When I'm Drunk\" and \"Herve Villechaize\". A little acoustic guitar comes into play for \"The Fool\", one that sounds like a reworking of Wonder Stuff's \"Golden Green\". The high notes are a bit of a problem in \"Bright Side\", otherwise it has a nice, light pop sound ala Greenberry Woods. \"Soaking In It\" has an interesting and distorted pop sound, if you can imagine Oingo Boingo at slightly less than normal speed. \"Handbag\" dispenses the jangly guitar sound for a heavier feel, overshadowing the vocals at times. \"What Do We Forget\" mixes a two-step drumbeat with more of the heavier guitars, and misses the mark as one of the more forgettable songs of this collection. \"Horseface\" and a demented version of Duran Duran's \"Sound Of Thunder\", continue the heavier sound, edging past the crash pop boundary into more of a punk tune, with the guitars and drums grinding out a mosh pit beat. One of the more interesting cuts here is \"The Ballad Of Bo And Luke\", a country ballad summing up the script of every episode of Dukes Of Hazzard. \"Steven Patrick Buzzwords\" is a sugary pop ballad, a bit out of place on this disc, but well done nonetheless. The Beach Boys, Don Ho, The Ventures, and every cheesy Mexican restaurant muzak soundtrack are blended into the odd closer \"Cha Cha Ho!\". This is a pretty wild and enjoyable ride overall. Even if the wheels seem to come off the tracks at times, they are righted quickly. Caroline Aiken is a well respected veteran of the folk scene in and around the Atlanta area, and this new release should do nothing but enhance her reputation. Typically, she relies on her acoustic guitar and vocals that range from soft and soothing to an occasional bite when dictated by the song. In a deceiving manner, \"Good Intentions\", a hard blues track, opens this disc. The title cut comes next, with help from fellow Atlanta folk legends Amy Ray and Emily Saliers of the Indigo Girls, with Ray occasionally taking the lead. The three voices fit together perfectly, especially near the end when the guitar rests and we're treated to pinpoint harmonies. \"Hotel At Highway One\" throws a nice soothing piano in a ballad with a hint of country. \"Ground Zero\" returns to the more familiar, guitar based folk, this one with Aiken alternating her vocal and guitar style from gritty and raw to gently soothing in the same song. There's more blues to be found in \"Love Ain't Going Nowhere\", with powerful vocals set to an acoustic track, and the electric \"Movin' On\". \"That's What I Heard\" and \"Heart Of Knowing\" are nice closers, guitar and piano based respectively, more of the nice mellow folk Aiken does best. I could really like this one if it weren't for the bluesy interjections that throw off the continuity a little. They're not bad songs, in fact they are done quite well, but on a disc made up of a mostly laid back folk style, they make it hard to stay in a good listening groove. But, since that's the worst I can say about this one, you should conclude that fans of simple singer/songwriter type folk should take note. Based on four songs, it's often hard to make a good assessment of a band's capabilities, but I'm pretty sure I like this band. A lot. Singer Lisa Matthews is the key here, with a voice that moves easily from song to song, style to style. Add to that a very clean production job, nice songwriting on the three originals, an awesome cover, and a song featured on my favorite TV show - Homicide - and you get four songs that leave you thirsting for more. \"Killing Time\" is the one that was featured on a December episode of Homicide, and it's what you would expect from a song from that show. An eerie rocker, with Willem Elzevir's violin providing the mood, and images of the opening credits start rolling through your head. Leslie Gore's \"You Don't Own Me\" is next, with Elzevir contributing nice mandolin and violin, and Matthews stealing the show again with her soulful vocals. My personal favorite is \"No Regrets\", a light rocker with a 50's feel, \"do-do\"'s and \"yeah-yeah\"'s, and a rhythm that begs you to sing along. The closer, \"Some Other Time\", is a carry over from their first release, a full length that is now on my must have list. It compares favorably to Lisa Loeb's material, with a light, airy pop feel that serves as the final tug on the reel, pulling you into the net, where escape is not an option. Unfortunately, as soon as you realize you're hooked, it's over. \"Maybe She Will\" is the title of their first release, and I'll be sure to look for it. Hopefully this won't be the last we hear from Love Riot."
    ],
