    "body": [
      "If the Cure had any balls, they'd sound like The Faint. In fact, they used to before discovering that by wussing up their sound, they could get some FM airplay. The styles of The Faint and early, early Cure are very similar, an off-kilter pop sound built around simple guitar playing, laced with feedback in the right places, vocals just off enough to grab your attention, and an odd, yet catchy style of songwriting that sometimes takes a few listens to really set into your brain. A couple cuts here were featured on the Saddle Creek Sampler compilation, and they were good choices to represent the overall sound of this release. Vocalist Todd Baechle could be a voice double for Robert Smith with his ability on first take to sound very much like Smith, but at the same time, handle the rough stuff. This disc is, for the most part, a little heavier than your standard Cure release, but still rooted in the basic pop genre, often filled with catchy hooks, but sometimes with guitar tracks that seem to come from all directions, meeting in the middle to form a noise pop sound that grates at your brain stem until it finds a cozy place to settle in and become a fixture. If you're fortunate enough to be able to sample this one before you decide to buy, give it a few listens. I almost wrote it off after the first couple, but as I mentioned, after a few, you'll probably become hooked. This New York band describes themselves as power pop, but there's more than that in this release, their third overall. The emphasis is on power here, and the pop part is secondary. Songwriter Edward Marshall has a knack for writing songs that can appeal to fans of any of the pop/rock genres, crossing boundaries without sounding out of place in any. \"Sick\" starts the disc, and is perhaps the one song that best fits the general power pop tag, with a driving guitar base that would put the song comfortably into the modern rock/MTV playlist. \"The Foreign Dime\" has a psychedelic pop sound, with swirling keyboards and wah-wah guitars. \"Jealousy\" is a wild free for all on the guitars, a perfect soundtrack for an afternoon spent at a local carny riding the Himalaya until they chase you away. \"Always High\" is a snappy pop song full of hooks and a keyboard wrapper, with lyrics that beg to explain the origin. \"Maybe\" and \"B.F.C.\" are catchy fuzz rockers ala Matthew Sweet, but with a bit heavier guitar pounding out the rhythm, the latter with piano sprinkled in the mix. There is nothing new or innovative here, but with a nice variety, it prevents the disc from getting stale after multiple listens as many of the newer bands tend to do. Worth a listen if you get a chance, no matter what flavor of rock your ears prefer. Over the course of seven years, through various partnerships known as Dream Horse and The Lorax, The Watsons have honed their folk rock sound into one just a tad outside of the typical coffeehouse band, both relaxing and invigorating at the same time. Founders Christy Claxton and Stacy Lieder offer contrasting vocal styles, merged into harmonies not unlike those of the Indigo Girls, with an edgy songwriting style that emulates that of Amy Ray and not Emily Saliers. There are exceptions, such as the soothing and gentle ballads \"One Brick At A Time\" and \"I've Been Fine\", the saga of one quick spark from an old flame. Meg Beatty on bass and James Downing on drums are not as prevalent as the acoustic guitars, but the full band is in gear in the more rock oriented \"Runaway Heart\" and \"Devil In Your Ear\", the latter being guilty of overuse on the harmonica to a certain extent. One of the two vocalists has a style that tends to get annoying after more than one listen in a short period of time, but overall this is filled with well written, heavy folk. I particularly like the use of the bass and drums, not detracting from the folk roots of the songs, but adding just the right touch to make this more than just your average guitar and vocal folk disc."
    ],
