    "body": [
      "Your faith in the major labels will get shot all to hell when you learn that Sony let this one get away. I probably need to say up front that I'm not a huge country fan, and my collection of country has shrunk to the three Blackhawk discs and one Radney Foster, but after moving to Georgia, it was pretty sizeable at one time. And I'm not interested in the stereotypical twang that is all too often associated with the country genre. This one fits into the category that has been called young country or country pop, where catchy hooks and melodies appeal to those who might sneak an occasional peak at CMT, yet still won't admit to actually liking country music. White is backed by an impressive cast of characters, starting with producer Pete Anderson, who has handled the same chore for Dwight Yoakam. The backing musicians include Anderson and the rest of Yoakam's band, and brief appearances by Marty Stuart and Jim Lauderdale. Yoakam himself appears in the duet \"It's Better This Way\". Lauderdale and Lucinda Williams, best known (by me anyway) for writing \"Passionate Kisses\" for Mary Chapin Carpenter, have both written songs in this collection as well. Finally we get to White, who is deserving of attention even without the stellar lineup behind her. She glides through the variety of styles here without missing a beat, without sounding out of place, and more importantly, without losing your interest. Listen to \"Why Do I Love You\" and tell me you don't love this voice. She belts out the vocals with more soul than normally afforded a country singer, and backed by Skip Edwards on the organ, it is the highlight of this disc. Another nice one is the rootsy \"I Doubt If It Does To You\", laced with mandolin and having a front porch kind of comfortable sound. The first of Williams' songs is \"I Just Wanted To See You So Bad\", with the same radio-friendly pop sound as the aforementioned Carpenter tune. The same can be said for the title cut and \"Too Big For This Town\", and it's probably no coincidence that two of these three made it into my top cuts list. There are a couple where the twang and drawl are more prevolent than others, but even then, it's not significant enough to be a factor for those turned off by sort of thing. If the recent success of groups like Smash Mouth and Sublime are any indication, today's musical youth is ready for a band named Bughead. Mixing a lethal batch of funk, reggae, ska, and an occasional punk/pop outburst, they generate an energetic sound that is sure to get you moving in one way or another, either tapping your feet while you listen at the office, or, depending on your alcoholic input, slamming against the walls at your local club. Take the power of Red Hot Chili Peppers and mix in the hooks of Goldfinger and you start to get an idea of what to expect here. The opener, \"Men And Poets\", is a good indication of what's ahead, starting with a driving guitar beat, shifting into a soft ska beat, moving back to the guitars, breaking for a little reggae, then mixing them all together towards the end. The always changing flavors of music here segue nicely, and you're never caught by surprise. Whether it's the funk/metal of \"Dark Island\" and \"Electric Heroin\", the catchy ska/pop of \"Wrong Side Of Town\" and \"Dead At The Fair\", or the nice reggae beat in \"Why Me Love?\", Bughead should please even the most finicky listener. This one was a tough one to write about, because it sounds a little different each time you listen. One time through it's a nice edgy 90's pop album, ala Matthew Sweet meets Soul Asylum, and the next time it's a 60's sounding airy rock sound, uncovered in the vaults next to the stacks of vinyl. John is a veteran of the St. Louis music scene, releasing his first works in 1987 as a member of The Stranded Lads. On this, his third solo release, he displays the songwriting skills of one with those ten years of experience. John walks us through the many flavors of his guitar style, from a jangly Matthew Sweet style in \"July Breeze\", to an all out Dead-like jam in \"Damn\". Many times here you'll hear a hint of psychedelic, White album era Beatles, oozing of Lennon qualities. One other worth noting is the mandolin tinged, friendly sound of \"A Bit Warm\", while one worth forgetting is \"Kerosene\", which brings back nightmares of cheesy 80's glam-metal. There's more to like here than dislike, and with its light rock/pop tour of the last twenty years of music, it should appeal to more than one generation."
    ],
