    "body": [
      "When I first put this one on for a spin, it kind of took me back to my childhood wearing the grooves out of my 45 of Barry McGuire's \"Eve Of Destruction\", as Denton's rough around the edges vocal style takes a cue from McGuire or Kris Kristofferson, dropped onto a midwest style roots/country music base that doesn't really fit into any particular musical genre. After a few listens through the three songs here, the style kind of grows on you, yet trying to sort out the sounds that may have influenced the songwriting remains difficult. Taking a look at some of the covers the band plays in its live show, you'll find an odd mix, including Eagles, Grateful Dead and Marshall Tucker Band, which could be a good representation of the music here, but look further and you'll also find Nirvana, REM and Pink Floyd, which really make you wonder about the spin these guys could put on such a diverse list. The key cut here, \"Parole Song\", is the best of the three, a poetic spin on the freedom and escape from reality Denton experienced while living in the Arizona desert. Written in a style similar to old Eagles, it features a country-tinged folk style that is finely complimented by background harmonies. \"Low-Fi Jesse\" reminds me a lot of Neil Young's \"Sugar Mountain\" in the music at times, and is a nice jangly, old time country-ish tapper. \"Sodbuster\" has a ragtime meets bluegrass feel with guitars instead of banjos and painos, another with a catchy rhythm. This one isn't the kind of music you'd normally find in my collection, but for a quick change of pace, it's a good listen. If you can honestly say that you don't like Roy Orbison, The Everly Brothers, and the modern genre known as Americana, you can turn back now. Those of you still reading, buy this disc. The Lonesome Strangers are Jeff Rymes and Randy Weeks, and after a two year break from the rigors of touring and recording, they're back with what should become a classic in today's roots rock revival. They've once again teamed up with producers Pete Anderson and Dusty Wakeman, known for their work with Dwight Yoakam among others, as well as the first Lonesome Strangers release some eleven years ago. The results are a tasty and fresh smorgasbord of jangly country, pop, folk, rock, and just about any combination of the above, and the vocals fit together like long lost pieces of a jigsaw puzzle. Comparisons to The Everly Brothers are inevitable, with the harmonies grabbing your attention in songs such as \"Sharon\" and \"And It Hurts\". On \"Ton Of Shame\", there's a folk/blues style reminiscent of early Steve Forbert. My personal favorite is \"Fine Way To Treat Me\", a rootsy pop song that falls somewhere between The Jayhawks and Nick Lowe. Marshall Crenshaw and Buddy Holly come to mind when listening to \"Lingerin' Thing\", a twang pop toe tapper that puts an edge on the late 50's rock and roll sound. The one low spot is a too-twangy cover of The Louvin Brothers' \"I Don't Believe You've Met My Baby\" that really seems out of place among the others here. Other than that, every song here is a keeper, and even with repeated listens, they don't lose their flavor. With three years of performing live shows behind them, the timing seems right for the debut full-length from Yuma House. This DC-area foursome has seemingly mastered the pop genre, with a nice mix of power, quirk, and noise pop, driven by a keen sense of catchy melodies no matter what the style. At times they remind me a lot of Bee Stung Lips with the simplistic, sometimes odd approach on songs such as \"Tara\", \"Annmarie\" and \"Lower Providence\". The comparisons move over to Greenberry Woods on the power pop songs like \"Sister Station\", a nice one with a little keyboard thrown into the mix. The guitars get a bigger role in the noisier pop ala Foo Fighters or Emote on \"Out Of My Hands\", a perfect track for modern radio, and \"Track 6, Track 7\", which borrows a familar guitar opener from U2. When they choose to, Yuma House can kick with the best of them, but this one is more of the melodic pop variety, heavy on flowing melodies, catchy hooks, and well written verses destined to get into your head. A debut deserving attention, and hopefully not the last we hear from this group."
    ],
