    "body": [
      "This duo was first heard from as part of Canada's Bourbon Tabernacle Choir, but after that band moved to New York to explore new ground, they ended up splintering apart in different directions, leaving Chris Brown and Kate Fenner working as a twosome. To describe their debut release, the logical first step would be to compare Brown and Fenner to Everything But The Girl, but there is much more here. Jazz and ambient musical backing, sometimes reaching to almost a loungy pop feel, provide a wonderful layer behind Fenner's soulful vocals. Brown takes over lead vocals on a couple of the cuts, but with mixed results. On \"Time (Murder Ballad)\", the harder style doesn't suit the overall tone of the disc, and he seems overmatched. But on \"Bleed\", where the pace is a little slower, he sounds much more comfortable, and with Fenner providing nice background and harmonies, the combination is real nice. Politically and socially conscious lyrics are sprinkled throughout, such as in \"Claim The Ashes\" and \"Whoever Built This Would Never Live Here\", but the laid back approach is kin to mixing the arsenic in with the kool-aid mix. I see this disc as nice background to any opportunity where the music is important to set a mood, but not take over the situation. It's relaxing and enjoyable, but without being overbearing. I'm not sure there's a clear genre where Farley would find a home, but if I were forced to categorize, I'd put her music just inside the edge of the folk circle. She'd be in the company of artists such as Ani DiFranco, Tori Amos, and Andrea Florian, where if you strip the music down to its barest form, it is clearly of a modern folk style, but the artist herself adds so much to that with an intriguing writing and/or delivery style. Farley combines a flair for well-placed musical accompaniment and lyrics that sometimes dare to be controversial, with a vocal style that moves from powerful to sensual to gentle with ease, weaving a magic spell with songs that speak of topics that hit the subjects of racism (the sad but true \"Carefully Taught\"), abuse (\"Daddy's Little Girl\") and materialism (\"A Better Haircut\"). Fans of early Amos should delight in this one, but only if you're not too attached to the piano. Farley relies primarily on an acoustic guitar, but sits down at a piano for \"Breathless\", and works the keyboards for \"The Best In Me\" and \"Carefully Taught\". Amos and DiFranco have proven that a market exists for this style of music, and that with or without commercial airplay, the buyers will find their way to the good music. Don't get lost along the way and miss this one. The nine songs here are perhaps the best example of versatility I've heard yet, with Andrews and her incredible voice dishing out a batch of originals and classics that dare to knock down categorical barriers. As she pays homage to the songs from the past, her originals follow the same path, winding through sounds of the classics from pop to country to blues. The title cut has a very 90's country/pop sound, clean and crisp much like something from a Mary Chapin Carpenter disc. \"Hands That Hold\" has a throwback country sound, but mixes in a little folk style harmonies ala Indigo Girls. \"Crocodile Tears\" is a strong ballad that is sure to bring on flashbacks to Patsy Cline, on which note I'll mention a couple covers here of Cline's material, \"Strange\" and \"Back In Baby's Arms\". Also included is a killer cover of \"I Can't Let Go\", done by many others including Evie Sands and The Hollies. You could probably argue that when you have a voice this powerful and flexible, you can make anything sound good, but Andrews proves with her originals that she can write a good tune too."
    ],
