    "body": [
      "If it's possible to wear out a CD, my copy of the Matchbox 20 debut is quickly heading in that direction. Luckily for me and the millions of others who have made Matchbox 20 international stars, Rob Thomas, Brian Yale, and Paul Doucette were responsible for vocals, bass, and drums respectively for this Florida band that was a well kept secret until recently. After a not so friendly split, varying stories regarding the record deal that led to Matchbox 20, and the subsequent lawsuit over the song \"3am\", these original recording are available for all to enjoy. This one is full of crisp, intelligent, and melodic rock anthems, bearing Thomas' distinctive vocal style on most, but there are some where I thought the recordings were made after his departure with a new singer. There are two versions of the almost overly popular \"3am\", two of the new single \"Forever December\", and nine others that can easily be dropped into almost any rock radio format. Overall, this one has a somewhat lighter feel to it than does the Matchbox 20 disc, but all the qualities that attracted the millions are still here. For the fans of the harder, modern rock sound, there are the songs such as \"High\", \"Unkind\", and \"Tired\" that bear that now unmistakable Thomas sound. For those more interested in the mellower, sometimes acoustic sound, be sure to check out \"Paint Me Blue\" and \"Dear Joan\". Either way, this is one of those must have discs for even the most finicky collections. More then twenty years after the suicide of Pete Ham, Badfinger's music has continued to live on in various compilations, rediscovered demo tapes, and solo efforts from the other members of the group. The latest is from drummer Mike Gibbins, who maintains a 60's or 70's sound on this release of new material that has more of a George Harrison sound than it does Badfinger. There are times when Gibbins falls into that dreaded \"too old too rock and roll\" rut, but he always rights himself with another nice ballad, or even the catchy \"Bad Boy Blues\", which although an odd inclusion here, is a nice diversion midway through the disc. \"Time In\" is one of the standouts, mixing one part Procol Harum and one part Supertramp into one of those cuts that keeps playing in your head long after you're done listening. \"Picture Of You\" is a nice piano and keyboard ballad and \"Layaway\", one of those with the Harrison guitar sound, has been mentioned as a tribute to former bandmate Pete Ham? Gibbins is best when the piano or keyboard is the focal instrument, and doesn't quite have the vocal strength to keep up with the more rocking cuts. For casual Badfinger fans this may be a little on the disappointing side, but Gibbins does a nice job of writing songs that stay rooted in the past without being stale, and letting the music itself take precedence over the production studio temptations. tsecret@magicnet.net forbidden@gowebway.com http://www.magicnet.net/rz/audio/tsecret http://www.forbiddenrecords.com"
    ],
