    "body": [
      "I must preface this review with a bit of explanation. I gave up on heavy metal long ago, when I discovered college radio in the 80's. So when I saw this one arrive, I'll admit, I was a little bit skeptical. But I'll try anything, so I put it on for a listen. I was fully expecting to pass on it and move on, but I can honestly say I was pleasantly surprised. Gillan has spent his life in music building a reputation as a classic heavy metal screamer, catching his fame as vocalist for Deep Purple, not to mention a successful solo career, a stint with Black Sabbath, and now back with the reunited Purple. He describes this release as \"background music\", but if you leave it at that, you'll miss a lot of the great musicianship to be found here. I'm not at all familiar with Gillan's recent work, so longtime fans may roll their eyes as I play dumb, but the casual fan can equate this disc with Robert Plant's venture away from the norm with the Honeydrippers. I fully expected to get the same old wailing guitars and vocals, and there are couple here that almost fit into that category, but this is really a nice, mellow disc. Oddly enough, \"Hard On You\", one of the ones I selected as a top cut is one of the harder ones, but with one of those catchy rhythms that keeps playing in your head. These heavy guitar cuts are wrapped up early on, and then the disc takes on a light, airy feel with the use of pan flutes and other interesting sounding instruments. \"Country Mile\" is a gentle acoustic number that has nice guitar work that will bring back memories of old George Harrison solo material. There's a loungy blues number (\"You Sold My Love For A Song\"), a finger snapping ditty with an environmental story (\"That's Why God Is Singing The Blues\"), even a little celtic sounding drinking song about his relationship with his dad (\"Gunga Din\"), and a little of everything else, most of which will be unexpected. The Honeydrippers reference came primarily from the disc's closer, \"Anyway You Want Me\", which is a neat 50's style crooner. No matter how you look at it, Gillan's work here with Steve Morris is exceptional, not only for the writing and playing, but for the creativity shown in a break from the norm. Not too many veteran artists would dare take these risks, much less pull it off with such flair as Gillan does here. Mike Estep has taken his stereotypical singer/songwriter product, one that he has spent close to ten years performing in central New York area, and beefed it up with a full band for his debut release. The result is something more than your average folk/rock product, a very nice light rock release that compares favorably to something along the lines of Bruce Hornsby or Marc Cohn. Based on an acoustic guitar, the sound is built up with help from area performers, including Gary Frenay (see \"Armory Square\" ). The songs here are solid and well written, mini stories wrapped in music with subjects such as the Vietnam memorial wall, corporate downsizing in a small town, and the walk down death row. Reading through the lyrics brings back memories of early Springsteen, where the music sometimes seemed secondary, the story of the song being the focal point. Kudos to Estep for coming up with the right ingredients for an excellent adult-rock disc, and at the same time stirring the mix just enough so that it isn't confined to that one audience. While there isn't that one standout cut, there isn't a bummer in the bunch. Graham grew up learning to play songs by folk/rock legends Joni Mitchell, James Taylor, and Jackson Browne, and was later influenced by rock artists such as The Stones and Elvis Costello, and the diverse mind set has resulted in a nice clean folk/pop disc that has all the qualities that made early 10,000 Maniacs so listener friendly. Graham has written all the songs here, and they range from a soft folk sound to a full band pop sound, most in the latter category, but all well written and performed. Graham's voice is sure to bring comparisons to Natalie Merchant, but fortunately she lacks the sometimes shallow and meek delivery style that Merchant often exhibited. Graham sings with authority and confidence, and that elevates her songs over those by Merchant. There's a little bit of Mary Chapin Carpenter here too, in the manner that Carpenter doesn't necessarily fit into any one genre, mixing in hints of folk, pop, country, resulting in songs that appeal to a wide audience. The same is true here, and Graham deserves the recognition already afforded to these better known artists based on the collection here."
    ],
