    "body": [
      "In a recent interview, Mike Arnold, vocalist for Tiny Red Spiders stated that the band liked \"to keep the audience off guard by keeping things diverse\", and their debut release proves that they can indeed do just that. Taken too far, diversity can be a bad thing, but on this release, the band stays true to the modern heavy alternative format, but mixes it up enough to attain their goal of keeping things far from boring. Arnold assumes a spot in elite vocal company with a strong performance, and with some great playing behind him, this band soon should have no problem finding life outside Springfield can be grand. Although at times the music can get a little too metalish for my tastes, there is still plenty to rave about in the ten songs here. If you can imagine Oingo Boingo's \"Only A Lad\" remixed with the distinctive guitar noise prevalent in the heavy rock movement of today, you'll get \"Crazy Thelma\", my favorite of this collection. A little more towards the middle of the road than most, but still hard enough to please the discriminating young listener. \"Mallory\" changes gears more than once, starting as a haunting ballad, then taking a schizophrenic ride through guitar rhythms and solos wrapped around a great bass line. I don't necessarily like all of this, but I have to be fair and say that it is at least as good, if not better, than what I'm hearing on radio and seeing on the charts lately. All you modern alterna-rock fans and media should eat this one up. When I reviewed the debut from Nora's Breakfast Club ( \"Soliloquy\" ), I left it with a \"to be continued\" tone, lots of potential, but not quite there yet. Here's the follow-up, and the story has a happy ending. This one is more of the cloudy pop style that the debut featured, but the overall tone of the writing style has picked up a bit to where the band is leaning more toward a power pop style, yet still maintaining the basic sound that has now come to define the band. The guitars are louder than your normal pop band at times, leading to more of an alternative rock sound, but make no mistake, this is a pop disc. And a fine one at that. Vocalist Danny Sharrett has an interesting delivery style, somewhat slurred, yet understandable, and he maintains an upbeat tone that sits well on top of the jangly rhythms that form the underlying feel-good attitude that this one exudes. Josh Britt takes over lead vocals on \"I'll Be Gone\" and the band becomes a Smiths sound alike, if you can imagine Morrissey in a good mood for a change. I'll use Ocean Blue as a reference point again, throwing Blue Cartoon into the mix as well, something a little different for the power pop crew. Two songs each from a couple of NJ/PA area bands, the first (and better) of which is The Unmarked Cars, with a well honed ska/core sound. You'll be even more impressed once you find out they've been together only around two years. The first one, \"2U\" is a great raucous horn backed, must-dance-to rocker, while \"Stupid Girl Skank\" slows it down and relies more on the horns to carry the tune. Both fun and well done. Supergrub is more of a mystery based on the two cuts here. First up is \"Building The Bridge\", a likeable mix of early Police with extra bass and a more modern progressive rock sound. Their other cut is \"Destitute\", a classic reggae ditty, and although they both are also well done, they leave me curious about what to expect from a full length release. No doubt some talent here, let's hear more!"
    ],
