    "body": [
      "This is now the third release from Astro Chicken that I've had the pleasure of reviewing (see demo and Disposable ), and when I heard a new one was coming, I was disappointed every day I'd check the mail and it wasn't there. The wait finally ended, and after some initial disappointment, it turned out to be worth it. For some reason, maybe the high anticipation, it took a few listens for the songs to settle, but this latest one just gets better and better with every listen. The band has expanded on their prototypical power pop sound a bit with some fine results, livening up the disc for a spin that will be anything but boring. It takes a couple cuts for the disc to find a good groove, but \"Germs\" will get your toes tapping with it's harmonica and hooks, then \"Everything\" turns it up a notch, after a slow start, into a wake up call for the rest of the disc with a very unlikely heavy guitar base. One of the highlights here is \"Honeymoon\", a very Jellyfish-like song, absolutely fun to listen to over and over. \"If You Want It\" borrows a little style from Badfinger and The Beatles, mixing in a distorted vocal track with a familiar musical bed. \"Neverwill\" is a repeat from the last release, harder in style than most, with the feel of a Soul Asylum song. \"Nothing Around For Me\" and \"Beatrice Is Beautiful\" are a jangly, acoustic numbers featuring nice harmonies on vocals, the kind of song that makes this band so appealing. There is some really great stuff mixed in here, and those that don't quite merit such praise are more than just filler. This course in modern power pop does have three prerequisites, those being prior enjoyment of late era, psychedelic Beatles, at least a familiarity with early pop pioneers such as Badfinger or Big Star, and a keen sense of the trippy pop of Jellyfish. With those things behind you, this one will seem like a natural progression in your pop education. It's really amazing that these guys aren't signed somewhere already, and I still believe it is just a matter of time. The easiest way to get at me is a good power pop disc, and here's another decent one from Arizona's 9-Volt. Guitarist and vocalist Andy Mitchell and bassist Jeff Criswell have been working together in various projects since the early 90's, and they recruited drummer Scott Collins for this release. Unfortunately for the band, Criswell left just after recording was complete, but he has already been replaced for future work by Steve Ramaglio. What makes this one a bit different from your average power pop disc is that the band doesn't stick with the same formula for all the selections, providing a few of the generic alternative variety, heavier on the guitars and without the hooks and melodies of the typical power pop genre. As a split, it runs around 50/50 between the two, and the placement runs random, so there's no telling what's next at any given time. This is unfortunate because the band is good either way, but I have to be in a different mindset for each. The two are very distinct and separate styles, and for me don't blend together well. If you follow the reviews here, you've probably figured out that I'm a power pop fanatic, so I naturally gravitate towards those cuts here, and the top cuts list reflects that preference, except for maybe \"Headstrong\". Overall, the band is solid, and with good production by Gary Loizzo, who has worked with Styx and REO Speedwagon, they provide ample fodder for modern radio and power pop fans alike. Maybe for commercial success, the mix will turn out to be good strategy, and if so, it'll be a bonus for fans of both sides. A short but sweet debut from this new west coast ska/punk band, made up of members from Skankin Pickle and Rudiments. This lineup actually toured Europe under the Skankin Pickle name, and the familiarity bodes well for this six song, twelve minute introduction to the band. Bouncing from a pop to a punk spin on the traditional ska sound, they maintain a steady level of energy throughout. The disc starts with the radio friendly \"The Longest Way Home\" and the pace picks up from there hitting a frantic beat for the two cuts in the middle of the disc, then starts to settle back down with \"Pawn Takes King\", a quirky ska/pop cut where the horns stand out more than the others here. And as with every good presentation, \"Got Nothin'\" wraps things up with a fairly comprehensive summation of the band's agenda, whipping through a recap of the various styles and speeds. Lots of promise for this new lineup, and lots of fun for you should you choose to grab the disc. A US tour is in the works, as is a full length release. Watch for both."
    ],
