    "body": [
      "Back in the early 80's when 2-Tone ska was starting to put a minor dent on the college music scene here in the states with such bands as Madness, The Specials, and The [English] Beat, there was another band getting things together. Rob \"Bucket\" Hingley had just relocated from The UK to New York City and was somewhat surprised to find that ska hadn't had the same impact here as in his native country. So with a few buddies, he put a band together to see what he could do to change the ska scene. All these years and lineup changes later, The Toasters are still doing their thing, long after those others have come and gone (and in some cases come back). Since their first single in 1983, The Toasters have amassed a lengthy discography, including a few produced by Joe Jackson, on their way to becoming one of the few long term contributors to the ska movement. On their latest, you certainly get your money's worth, with a hefty seventeen songs, all of which showcase the energetic dance style of ska that Hingley and crew have come to master over the years. To keep things interesting, there's a good bit of variation, including a more defined reggae and pop beat in \"Daddy Cry\", a remake of the Spencer Davis classic \"Gimme Some Lovin'\" (tough to redo a classic and at the same time change the style of song), a bop/jazz style in \"Today's A Good Day\", a great blues/swing beat in \"Rude Rude Baby\" and \"Bye Bye Baby\". The only really forgettable track is \"Woyay\", a spastic reggae/punk cut that sounds like Steve Miller trying to rip off some old Police music. Other than that one deviation, this is grade A, must have for any 2-Tone fan, and for all you johnny-come-lately fans of bands like Goldfinger, Bosstones, and 311, this is where it all started coming together for this new wave of ska/core/punk types. See why the years haven't taken their toll on The Toasters. Welcome to the NYC Ska scene, courtesy of Moon Ska Records, and this fine compilation of city bands with unreleased cuts from staples such as The Scofflaws and The Toasters and a good sampling of new bands just hitting the scene. This disc should appeal to both the casual ska fan, as well as the devoted follower, with 22 bands featured, totaling a generous 77 minutes. The selections here feature the full spectrum of sounds that fall under the ska umbrella, including oldschool and reggae-tinged ska, some 80's 2-Tone dance that introduced ska to the masses with bands like Madness and The Specials, and some current ska/core style that seems to be taking off in the clubs and modern rock radio. A lot of this particular disc is concentrated on the older styles, where the pace isn't quite as frantic as newcomers might be used to, and the horns bridge a fine line between ska and an improv jazz sound. I personally like the more upbeat style, which makes up maybe a third of this one, but there's a few with a reggae beat that are pretty enjoyable as well. This is a good disc to get your feet wet in the ska movement, get used to the different styles, and hopefully go check out some of these new bands to fill out that ska section of your collection. This one is the studio half of a simultaneous two disc release from New York's Tadpoles, and is the third one produced by Bongwater's Kramer. One way to describe their style would be acid-rock for the 90's generation. They put an experimental edge on the classic sound of modern rock, much the same way Jefferson Airplane and the like did in the 60's. The musical base is an early 90's, pre-shoegaze sound, built up with a wall-of-sound production style that adds some meat to an already hefty arrangement. If I had to dig something out of the bag to use as a comparison, I'd probably have to go with a heavier reincarnation of House Of Love. Before the discovery of grunge, this is the kind of stuff that dominated alternative radio stations, so there's nothing new here, but with a little feedback here and there, some wild guitar work, and real nice production, those of you wanting a familiar sound from the not too distant past might want to check this one out. And if you buy this studio disc from the Bakery Records web site, they'll throw in a special live disc from this same band from the recent Terrastock festival."
    ],
