    "body": [
      "The three principle members of Calaveras sound as if they were put on this earth to make music together. The group is made up of Victoria Blythe, master of many instruments and beautiful vocalist, Greg Beattie, who handles vocals, guitar, bass, and percussion, and Dave Decker on guitar, mandolin, vocals, bass and percussion. With help from other bay area musicians, they have come up with a mix of traditional and modern folk sounds that has certainly caught my attention, in a genre that I don't usually get too excited about. I can compare them to other indie faves Orphan Moon with the elaborate yet gentle songwriting style and The Westerleys for the fine rotating vocal duties. Beattie and Blythe are the main vocalists, with Beattie having a very James Taylor sounding voice and Blythe reminding me of Alison Krauss at times, but in a league of her own. Her range is put to the test on \"Lullaby\" and she passes with flying colors, as her vocals carry the song, a gentle tribute that is one of the standouts here. \"A Street Called Alice\" finds Beattie on vocals, and is written and performed in a style that could pass for early Taylor, soothing in both the instruments and vocals, where one of his others, \"The Time Is Now\" has a more late model Taylor sound with the band getting a little more focus. All three share lead and backup vocals on \"Ready To Fly\", and the mix is incredible, certainly the highlight of the bunch, even though it is hard to pick just one or two favorites. Even if there are a couple of cases where they'll turn things up a bit into a light rock sound, Calaveras should appeal more to the folk crowd, both young and old. Music this good deserves your attention, even if the genre isn't exactly your favorite. If there is even a passing interest, you owe it to yourself to at least check them out. I can't help but think how this band would fit nicely as one of the local features on the Lilith Fair stage, presenting an eclectic folk/rock style based on acoustic guitar, but mixing in the other standards for the full band sound. The first couple listens you'll probably spend getting used to the vocal style of Ashley Gough, who has that unpolished feel of Jewel or Alanis Morissette, but with a somewhat rougher style. One time through, you'll be real happy with her work, but the next time not. The songs themselves are nicely written and played, woven around Erik Erickson's acoustic guitar, occasional harmonies mixed into the catchy folk which ranges from a harder rock beat all the way down to a stripped down coffeehouse feel. \"Hey Sadie\" is the highlight, featuring a beat that is guaranteed to have you tapping along. Gough seems more at ease with the slower tunes, and when things pick up a bit, she seems overmatched, such as in \"Finer Things\". \"Buddha\" is the exception to that rule though, with a light blues sound and Gough exhibiting perhaps her best work. I like the storytelling side of \"Grayson\", but it is another where the vocals are a tad detrimental to the quality of the song. Overall, an A for effort and songwriting, but the unsteadiness of the vocals does end up knocking the final grade down, but just a tad. There's a certain aura of goofiness about this one, with spaghetti covered subjects as cover art and a leadoff song that hints at a less-than-serious approach. Often times that would lead to certain death before reaching the end of the disc, but this one survives and even thrives on a crisp pop sound mixed with a quirky approach, sort of like Blotto jamming with Josie Cotton or Belinda Carlisle. Leaders Dawn Redmann and Brent Wagner write the kind of songs that are still playing in your head long after you're done listening, with a range of styles to choose from. The one that bounces around my head is \"Yesterday Today And Tomorrow\", a perfect candidate for airdrums or dancing. Redmann handles just about all the lead vocal chores, but Wagner steps in for a turn in \"Shattered Kisses\", another of the catchy selections. Her vocals are great, and you get the feeling that she would flourish with any style of music behind her, but this may be the perfect marriage of pop and vocal style. You'll quickly adjust to the off-center delivery, and just have a good time listening. You can't help it."
    ],
