    "body": [
      "I can easily name this one as my favorite of the month, and possibly one of the top two or three of the year so far. It wasn't until I started reading through the paperwork that accompanied the disc that I started understanding why this one is so good. The Andersons are Derrick, Bill, Bob, and Jimmy, although only Derrick can lay true claim to the surname. His past work includes a stint in Chewy Marble with Wondermint's Brian Kassan, the Pete Ham tribute \"7 Park Avenue\", and a band from the early 90's named The Living End. Bob is actually Robbie Rist, known for his work in Wonderboy and Martin Luther Lennon (another personal favorite). Jimmy has played with The Muffs, Dave Davies, and Green Jello. From some of the bands mentioned above, you can probably guess that this one is another in the growing genre of power pop. However, The Andersons are destined to be more than just a few new faces in an already crowded room. The ultra-catchy feel and sing-along harmonies of \"Hey Coelacanth\" and \"From The Get-Go\" should reel in the classic power pop fans, while those seeking some red meat with their serving might prefer the dark, smoky style of \"In This World\" and \"The Truth Is Out\", both of which remind me a lot of Smithereens material. \"You'll Get Yours Yet\" has the distinctive sneer of an Elvis Costello song, and \"(I'm In Love With A) TV Girl\" has the hand claps and light country-pop sound of old Nick Lowe. My car has a built-in CD holder that can fit four, and the slots are usually occupied by one the two Greenberry Woods discs, Buffy by Who Hit John, Matthew Sweet's Earth disc, and a homemade comp of some of the bands I've reviewed. One of those four has to go to make room for this one, it's that good. Narrowing the top cuts list down to four was extremely difficult, as every one here is worthy, but I think the hardest part of this review will be to actually take the disc out of the player to move on to the next one. Power pop fans - waste no time in adding this to your collection. Formerly known as Skuttlebutt, the band decided on the name change to signify a break from their past, as well as a new level of maturity as a group. They call themselves a modern rock band, but I wouldn't be surprised to hear them classified in an electric folk manner. Vocalist and guitarist Tara Lane mixes her acoustic guitar into almost all the songs to give the sound a comfortable feel, more of an adult rock sound, even though the opportunity to turn it up a bit presents itself often. She seems at home singing each of the different styles and tempos, and proves herself worthy of national attention with a strong performance. Her Indigo Girls influence is evident in the acoustic and vocal driven \"The Wake\", but even more so in the jangly folk cut \"Fading Past\", complete with really nice harmonies and Scott Gordon's dulcimer. You'll catch a dose of catchy quirk/pop as well with \"Salt 'n' Vinegar\" and \"Sink and Shock\", two of the better cuts in this collection. They've had success on a local level, finishing as one of the finalists of 250 entries in a '97 contest, and it may just be a matter of time before that success spreads to a much larger degree. Let me start by saying this is nowhere near what I expected from the name, so don't let that be a turn off if you've gotten this far. These six guys from Brooklyn do play hard, fast, and loud, but with a flair for writing songs that should please a wide audience. In general, it's easy to compare The Heaves to Social D, with the thundering guitar and drums leading the way, and a melodic style that adds to the appeal. Dig a little deeper under the surface and you'll be impressed how they can mix up the sounds without detracting from the tone of the disc. Of course, there's the mandatory mellow cut, \"The Only One\", nicely done as well, but that's the exception here. That one aside, these guys could be to the modern rock movement what the Ramones were to the late 70's and early 80's. Heads-down jams with interesting twists and turns, such as the interesting country-punk of \"Hard Lovin' You\", the classic crash pop of \"Acid Rain\" and the r-rated, anti-yuppie \"Brooklyn, NY\" and their less-filling hardcore \"She's A Bitch\". It took ten years for the band to get around to putting out their first disc, let's hope the next one comes a lot sooner than that."
    ],
