    "body": [
      "This disc is a sampler of some of the available recordings by Nenad Bach, a Croatian born artist in search of success on this side of the planet. In Europe, Bach has had two number one albums among his credits, and makes his American debut with \"I Love Losers\", featuring guest appearance by members of The Band, The Grateful Dead, and The Joe Jackson Band. His resume is impressive, including appearances with the likes of Bono, Meatloaf, and Pavarotti in the Concert For The Children Of Bosnia, and many TV and film soundtracks, including the recent score for the AMC movie \"The King Of Cool\". Showing a tremendous amount of variety and songwriting skills, the selections here, all but one of which are from the previously mentioned CD, range from ballads to rockers, and just about everything in between, even a short spoken word track, \"Peace\", featuring Martin Sheen. \"WWW/WWW (We Will Win On A Wild World Web)\" is a driving guitar rocker, the exception in this group of light pop selections. \"Can We Go Higher?\" features outstanding backup vocals, almost giving it a gospel feel. \"If I Had Another Life (I Love Losers)\" is an acoustic toe tapper, declaring Jerry Garcia for Secretary Of State among other entertaining lyrics. The ninth track, title unlisted, and \"Between Two Flights\" are nice ballads that might get hurt a bit by Bach's accent, which while not too bad, is more evident on the slower material. The final song, another unlisted one, is the winner here, a Buffet and world music mix that closes this particular disc on a fine upbeat note. Lots of potential here for American success, but I'm not entirely convinced it's as a singer. Again, the accent isn't all that pronounced, but I think Bach's success could come from writing songs that others end up performing. It's definitely worth a trip to his web site for a sampling. Leon Milmore is not a person, at least not one in this band, but a combination of the names of the four members - Lee Brooks, John Nolen, Troy Miller, and Chris Moore. It is that same theory of four makes one that lends itself to the cohesiveness demonstrated on their first full-length release. This band is not just another everyday acoustic pop band from the mid-Atlantic region that spawned the likes of Dave Matthews, and they've proven that by winning a local Decent Exposure contest, besting over 100 other contestants. The hefty cash prize afforded the band the ability to complete their own recording studio where this release was put together. While the band is sure to draw comparisons to Matthews, I hear more of a Badlees sound, with a friendly backyard party sort of pop style that you can't help but like. At times, Nolen's deep vocals strike up an image of Crash Test Dummies' vocalist Brad Roberts, but don't be alarmed as they don't have that same grate on your nerves quality found with CTD. Outside of the warm acoustic pop sound, there's \"So Happy\" and \"Grafitti Bridge\", heavier cuts featuring some electric guitar jamming laid over the acoustic sound. A little funky beat mixed with a psychedelic guitar solo marks the flavorful \"Spaceman\". Things get a little somber in \"Sleep\", with lyrics that could represent many scenarios. You'll like this one, and if I gave money back guarantees, this one would rank in that category. Those who enjoy DMB and/or The Badlees should be doubly pleased with this fine debut. Like long lost gems in a forgotten safe deposit box, Marshall Crenshaw has dusted off a collection of home made demos from the late seventies and early eighties. While some of this material has been available in a more polished version on several of Crenshaw's studio (and one live) releases, b-sides, and the promo disc \"A Collection\" from 1991, they are presented here in their raw format, most recorded on a living room 4-track. Although I'd imagine this collection aimed at the Crenshaw fanatic (like myself), it should appeal to anyone with even a passing interest in Crenshaw's classic Buddy Holly meets power pop sound, as long as you have an appreciation for the music and not just the production studio. Here you'll find original versions of Crenshaw classics such as \"Someday Someway\" and \"Rockin' Around In NYC\", the demo that turned into \"Bad Luck\" by Was (Not Was), an on-air version of \"That's It, I Quit\", an alternate version of his first single release entitled \"Something's Gonna Happen\", and a version of Bo Diddley's \"I'm Sorry\". A nice addition to the package are the liner notes, where Crenshaw gives us the story behind the songs. Don't be scared by the thought of a 4-track recording, the quality is better than you might think, and this disc will give you yet another, or at least the first, prize in your Crenshaw collection."
    ],
