    "body": [
      "Listening to this one from New Jesery's Thom MacFarlane, one can't help but feel part of a musical tour of the last two or three decades of pop music. For the most part, this is MacFarlane alone, centered around his acoustic guitar and vocals. He can be a one man band, adding bass, drums, piano, keyboards, harmonica, and lute to his list of credits, or get a dose of help from others (most notably Clem Burke of Blondie and Mike Grau of The Marbles ). Musically, MacFarlane is a master writer, with a knack for taking a familiar sound and turning it into something distinctively his own. He eludes any specific genre by zigzagging across the musical map. The first song on the disc is \"Fascist Fingers\", a playful pop song ala Greenberry Woods, and that's followed by \"Turn Away\", with a Byrds or Turtles retro pop sound. My favorite is next, \"In Between The Sheets\", which should be paying royalties to Elvis Costello with its \"Oliver's Army\" piano pop sound and ending up with a finish borrowed from \"Accidents Will Happen\". The tone switches back over to the 60's sound on \"Hey Casey\" and \"Jesus Was An American\", both folky Dylanesque tunes with harmonica accents. Float back to the present for \"Mary\", another of the upbeat janlgy pop variety, and \"You Never Let Me In\", a battle of Matthew Sweet versus The Beatles. \"Long Time\" has a Woodstock era folk feel, and to stay in that era, he adds a cover of Joni Mitchell's \"Edith And The Kingpin\" near the end. The icing on the cake is the disc's closer, a piano composition titled \"Song For Judith\", written in a style very similar to older George Winston material. Simply stated, this is a marvelous collection, and with a new one already in the works, MacFarlane is an artist to watch. Originally released in Europe on the CMP label, Andy Rinehart is promoting and marketing this one himself here in the US. Featuring a potpourri of progressive rock laced with world music and touches of jazzy arrangements, it makes for a nice listen. There are a lot of comparisons to be made for the selections here, and although there's not a perfect match for the whole disc, you'll hear strains of late model Yes and Jon Anderson, Genesis and Peter Gabriel, and an occasional King Crimson or Fripp/Eno type arrangement. \"Being Wrong\" is a freindly Jon Anderson type pop song then \"Stone Diamond\" takes on an older Peter Gabriel feel. My personal favorite, \"Joan's Bones\", has an airy Paul Simon on the islands feel, even if the lyrics make for a Jeffrey Dahmer theme song. The Fripp/Eno comparisons come mainly from \"Fortune Cookie Dinner\", one of the heavier ones here, and the funky \"Book Of Rules\". Classify this one under progressive or world pop, a clean and crisp spin put on a tried and true style, one that should appeal to those raised on the sounds I used for comparison, but also to those looking for a light supplement to their modern rock collection. ...release, Dim's debut album on Park Way Records, Is ten songs that try to reveal a serious, dismal,and spiritualized look into the lives of it's members, but unfortunately falls short of ant true originality or ground breaking artistry. ...release is an album of slow brooding, radio friendly, songs that would probably be heard late night in a small, dark,secluded cafe, as you sip your latte, ridiculing your friends in your mind, thinking how your better than they are, and how no one understands you. The music is deep, dark,and very guitar driven. Many eighties influences can be heard in their music, most notably the pretenders. And that;'s not just because the vocalist sounds like Chrissie Hynde. \"Forbidden passion, yearning, remorse, despair, fear and hope\" are a few words that have been used to describe the band and their music. Yes it's true, it's all there, but I've heard it all before. Don't get me wrong, (No pun intended) there are two songs that I feel deserve special attention, \"Occasional Rain\" and \"No Longer A Mother\". So in conclusion, if you like the slow sorrowful sounds of the Pretenders than this album might be worth your time."
    ],
